<p>You don't need an account to start, and there's nothing to install. Sign up when you want to keep your game. Players you invite never need an account or to pay: they join from a link in their browser, on a laptop, desktop, iPad or phone.</p>`},{id:"step-1",heading:"1/ Bring in your cards",html:`
<p>Press ${Dt("New game")} at the top of the sidebar. It asks where your cards are now (signed out, press ${Dt(K0.label)} on <a href="/">prototable.gg</a>, and ${Dt("Build your game")} asks the same at your table):</p>
<div class="ways">
  <div class="way"><b>From Tabletop Simulator</b><span>Bring in your deck's card sheet, the image with every card on it. Each card is cut out, ready to play.</span></div>
  <div class="way"><b>From a spreadsheet</b><span>One row per card. Upload a CSV or paste from Google Sheets or Excel, and your columns become card fields.</span></div>
  <div class="way"><b>From scratch</b><span>Start a card and write it. Every card after it follows the same layout.</span></div>
</div>
<p>A spreadsheet is the quickest way to change many cards later. Put one card on each row and one field in each column:</p>
${YG()}
<p>Already drew your cards as images? Choose card images. Want to see the table first? ${Dt("Explore")} has poker, blackjack, Hearts, chess, checkers and Crazy Eights to play. Every way in, step by step: <a href="/help/adding-cards">Adding cards</a>.</p>
<p>A new game opens on its ${Dt("Overview")}: a checklist from your first card to playing it with a friend, each step ticking itself off as you go.</p>`},{id:"step-2",heading:"2/ Lay them out and set the table",html:`
<p>On your game's ${Dt("Cards")} page, ${Dt("Edit template")} places your fields (name, cost, type, text, art) on the card once, with your own fonts, colors, icons, border and back. Every card made from that template follows it, so moving the cost to the other corner moves it on every card. More in <a href="/help/templates">Card templates</a>.</p>
<p>Make a deck in ${Dt("Decks")}: a list of cards and how many of each. To lay out the table, press ${Dt("Edit")} at the table (<kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>E</kbd>) and add piles, lanes, mats, dice, tokens and counters from ${Dt("Add")}. Write your rules on the ${Dt("Rules")} page; players read them in the rulebook at the table.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><b>Try it alone first.</b> Choose ${Dt("Set up stand-ins")} to fill the other seats and play every one yourself, switching between them. You'll find the rules that don't work before anyone else does.</p></div>`},{id:"step-3",heading:"3/ Invite players and press Record",html:`
<p>Sign in, then press ${Dt("Share")} (at the top of every page, and at the table), choose who can join, and press ${Dt("Copy the invite link")}:</p>
<ul class="plain">
  <li><b>Open</b>: anyone with the link walks straight in.</li>
  <li><b>PIN</b>: they need the PIN as well as the link.</li>
  <li><b>Approval</b>: they knock, and you let them in.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each player gets a seat and a hand for hidden cards. Press ${Dt("Record")} when the game starts.</p>
<p>When a card is clearly wrong, edit it mid-game: every copy updates at once, on the table, in hands and in decks. Right-click a card and choose ${Dt("Add note")} to write down what happened. More in <a href="/help/playtesting-with-others">Playtesting with others</a>.</p>`},{id:"step-4",heading:"4/ Review, then play again",html:`
<p>Open your game's ${Dt("Playtests")}. Every card edited during a recorded playtest waits under ${Dt("Card changes")}: ${Dt("Keep")} makes the change stick, ${Dt("Discard")} puts the card back. Read what players said, then play the next version. More in <a href="/help/recording-and-review">Recording and review</a>.</p>
<p>Everything about a game is one row of tabs under its name, and ${Dt("Make")}, the first button at your table's top left (or press M), holds the same things, with the table still in view beside it:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Tab</th><th scope="col">For</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><b>Overview</b></td><td>Your checklist, this week's changes and your plan</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Cards</b></td><td>Every card, its template, importing, printing and ideas</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Pieces</b></td><td>Boards, tokens and dice</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Decks</b></td><td>Which cards, how many of each, and deck lists from TCGs</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Rules</b></td><td>How it's played, and the turn's steps</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Playtests</b></td><td>Recordings, card changes and what players said</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>${Dt("Publish")}, beside ${Dt("Open table")}, sends the game out. <kbd>T</kbd> takes you back to your table from any page, <kbd>G</kbd> lists your games, and <kbd>H</kbd> opens Explore.</p>`},{id:"next",heading:"Next steps",html:`
<p>Each step above has a short tour inside the app that points at the real buttons on your own table. Open ${Dt("Learn")} to take one.</p>
<div class="next">
  <a href="/help/adding-cards"><b>Adding cards</b><span>Card sheets, spreadsheets, images and new cards</span></a>
  <a href="/help/templates"><b>Card templates</b><span>Fields, fonts, borders and the card back</span></a>
  <a href="/help/playtesting-with-others"><b>Playtesting with others</b><span>Invites, seats, hands and stand-ins</span></a>
  <a href="/help/recording-and-review"><b>Recording and review</b><span>Record, notes, Playtests and restoring a session</span></a>
</div>`}],faq:[["Do I need an account to use Prototable?","No. You can start your game without one and sign up when you want to keep it. Players you invite never need an account."],["Do my players have to pay or install anything?","No. Players join free from a link in their browser, on a laptop, desktop, iPad or phone."],["Is Prototable free?",`Yes, for ${gj()}, with ${cf("free")} players a room. Designer (${yA("designer")}) adds unlimited games, ${cf("paid1")} players a room and unlimited recorded playtests.`],["Can I bring in my Tabletop Simulator deck?","Yes. Choose a Tabletop Simulator deck in New game or Import, and each card on its card sheet is cut out, ready to play and to print."]]},{slug:"adding-cards",nav:"Adding cards",title:"Adding cards",pageTitle:"Adding cards to Prototable: card sheets, spreadsheets and images",description:"Bring cards into Prototable from a Tabletop Simulator card sheet, a CSV or Google Sheets, or a folder of card images, or write them by hand on the Cards page.",lede:"Every card in your game is on its Cards page, one row each. There are five ways to fill it: a Tabletop Simulator deck, a spreadsheet, card images, PDFs, or writing cards yourself.",updated:"2026-11-03",sections:[{id:"data",heading:"Where your cards live",html:`
<p>Open your game's ${Dt("Cards")} page. It lists every card in the game, one row each, grouped by template down the left, with a column for each field and an ${Dt("In play")} column for how many copies are on the table. Click a row to open the card beside the list, and click a cell to edit it in place.</p>
<p>${Dt("Import")}, in the sidebar or on the Cards page, opens all the ways in below. How many copies of a card a game uses is set in ${Dt("Decks")}, not on the card.</p>`},{id:"card-sheet",heading:"From a card sheet (Tabletop Simulator)",html:`
<p>A card sheet is one image with every card laid out in a grid, the way Tabletop Simulator stores a deck. In ${Dt("Import")}, open ${Dt("A Tabletop Simulator deck")} and choose the image (PNG, JPEG or WebP).</p>
<ol class="plain">
  <li>Set ${Dt("Across")} and ${Dt("Down")} to the grid, up to 12 each. ${Dt("TTS 10 × 7")} sets the usual Tabletop Simulator sheet: 69 cards, since the last cell is its hidden card.</li>
  <li>Check ${Dt("Cards on it")}, in case the last row isn't full.</li>
  <li>${Dt("Name them")} sets the start of each card's name, numbered from 1. It starts as the file's name.</li>
  <li>Press ${Dt("Import")}. Each cell becomes a card, read row by row. A sheet of wide cells is read as landscape cards.</li>
</ol>
<p>When it's done, ${Dt("Show them in Cards")} lists the new cards and ${Dt("Make a deck")} puts them in a deck. On the Designer plan, ${Dt("Read all")} turns the pictures into cards with fields you can edit.</p>`},{id:"spreadsheet",heading:"From a spreadsheet",html:`
<p>Put one card on each row and one field in each column, with the column names in the first row. Every card needs a name.</p>
${YG()}
<p>In ${Dt("Import")}, open ${Dt("A spreadsheet")}. Either ${Dt("Upload a CSV file")} (.csv, .tsv or .txt), paste a Google Sheets link, or copy the cells from Google Sheets or Excel, paste them, and press ${Dt("Parse")}. ${Dt("Integrations")} has a Google Sheets card that opens the same import.</p>
<ol class="plain">
  <li>Under ${Dt("Assign to template")}, pick the template the cards use. If none fits, ${Dt("New template from these columns")} makes one: the first column becomes the name and the others become text fields.</li>
  <li>Check ${Dt("Column mapping")}. Common names are matched for you: "card name" goes to Name, "mana" to Cost, "text" or "rules" to Effect Text, "attack" to Power. Pick ${Dt("Skip")} for a column you don't want.</li>
  <li>Step through the preview, then press ${Dt("Import")}.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><p><b>Importing again adds new cards.</b> It doesn't update the cards you imported last time, so delete the old ones first if you're replacing a set.</p></div>`},{id:"images",heading:"From card images",html:`
<p>Already made your cards as pictures? In ${Dt("Import")}, open ${Dt("Picture files")} and choose them, or drag them in. PNG, JPG, WEBP, GIF and SVG all work, up to 10 MB each, and each image becomes one card.</p>
<p>Cards are named from their file names, so <code>goblin_scout_01.png</code> becomes "goblin scout 01". You can rename any of them before importing. Images you already imported are skipped. The back comes from the template.</p>
<p>The free plan takes 70 images per import and 200 image uploads in all. Designer has no limit, and its ${Dt("Bulk importer")} matches a folder of images to the rows of a spreadsheet.</p>`},{id:"pdf",heading:"From PDFs",html:`
<p>Made your cards for print in Illustrator, InDesign or Affinity? In ${Dt("Import")}, open ${Dt("PDF import")} and choose them: one PDF per card, or one PDF with a card on each page. Each page becomes a card, named after its file, and pages with a grid of cards, like a print-and-play file, are cut into their cards.</p>
<p>Then say where the backs are. For a PDF printed double-sided, each card gets the page after it; otherwise select pages and give them a back, or upload the back on its own once the cards are in.</p>`},{id:"by-hand",heading:"Writing cards yourself",html:`
<p>On the ${Dt("Cards")} page, press ${Dt("New card")}. It makes a blank card and opens it: give it a name and fill in its fields. The card's template decides which fields it has and how it looks (see <a href="/help/templates">Card templates</a>). Changes save as you type, and any table using the game shows them.</p>
<p>Before a big change, ${Dt("Save a version")} keeps the card as it is. Open its saved versions and press ${Dt("Restore")} to go back.</p>`},{id:"on-table",heading:"Getting cards on the table",html:`
<p>At your table, press ${Dt("Make")} (or M), open ${Dt("Cards")}, open a card, set how many copies, and press ${Dt("Deal to table")}. For a whole game, make a deck in ${Dt("Decks")}: a list of cards and how many of each, which is what a pile starts with on the table and what gets printed. A deck list from Magic, Pok&eacute;mon, One Piece and other TCGs comes in with ${Dt("Import TCG")} on the Decks page.</p>`}],faq:[["What card sizes does Prototable support?","Poker (64 x 89 mm), Bridge (57 x 89 mm), Mini (44 x 64 mm), Tarot (70 x 121 mm), Square (89 x 89 mm), or any custom size from 20 to 200 mm a side, portrait or landscape."],["Can I paste straight from Google Sheets?","Yes. Paste the sheet’s link, or copy the cells, header row included, paste them into the spreadsheet import, and press Parse."],["How many cards can a game have?","As many as you like once you sign in. Only the free plan’s image uploads are limited: 70 images per import and 200 in all."],["Does a Tabletop Simulator import bring the card backs?","The card sheet brings the fronts. Set the back once on the template and every card uses it."]]},{slug:"templates",nav:"Card templates",title:"Card templates",pageTitle:"Card templates in Prototable: fields, fonts, borders and backs",description:"Design a card layout once in Prototable: card size, text and image fields, fonts, borders, background and the card back. Every card follows it.",lede:"A card template is the layout a card is made from: its size, where each field sits, how it looks, and its back. Lay it out once and every card made from it follows.",updated:"2026-11-03",sections:[{id:"open",heading:"Making a template",html:`
<p>On your game's ${Dt("Cards")} page, the templates are listed down the left, each with how many cards it makes. Pick one and press ${Dt("Edit template")}, or press ${Dt("New template")} and pick a card size: Poker, Bridge, Mini, Tarot or Square, or ${Dt("Custom")} for any width and height from 20 to 200 mm. ${Dt("Templates")}, beside ${Dt("Import")}, shows them all side by side.</p>
<p>A game can have as many templates as it needs, one for each kind of card. Cards using a deleted template move to your game's default template.</p>
<p>The template editor needs a larger screen than a phone.</p>`},{id:"fields",heading:"Fields",html:`
<p>A field is one thing printed on the card. Add one from the toolbar and drag it into place:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">Holds</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><b>Name</b></td><td>The card's name</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Text</b></td><td>A short line, like a type or a cost</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Number</b></td><td>A number, like power or health</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Text Block</b></td><td>Rules text that wraps over several lines</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Image</b></td><td>A picture, different on each card</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Icon</b></td><td>An icon from the library, chosen per card</td></tr>
    <tr><td><b>Shape</b></td><td>A rectangle, circle, triangle, star or heart</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Each field's label is its column on the Cards page, so a field called "Cost" is filled from each card's Cost. Click a field to set its font, size, weight, alignment, color, fill and border, or to lock it in place. Its ${Dt("Layer")} buttons move it in front of or behind other fields.</p>`},{id:"look",heading:"Border, background and fonts",html:`
<p>On the ${Dt("Front")} tab, ${Dt("Border")} sets the card's frame: ${Dt("None")}, a color, a gradient or an image, with its thickness and corner radius. ${Dt("Background")} is a color, or an image uploaded as the whole card's frame.</p>
<p>On the ${Dt("Settings")} tab you set the ${Dt("Card Size")}, ${Dt("Portrait")} or ${Dt("Landscape")}, the ${Dt("Default Font")} for every field, and a ${Dt("Foil")} finish. There are 30 fonts to choose from, grouped as sans, serif, display, handwriting and mono.</p>`},{id:"back",heading:"The card back",html:`
<p>The ${Dt("Back")} tab sets what the other side looks like, for every card made from the template:</p>
<ul class="plain">
  <li><b>Design</b>: a back built like the front, with its own border and background.</li>
  <li><b>Image</b>: one picture for every back.</li>
  <li><b>Double</b>: a double-sided card, with its own fields on the back that change card by card.</li>
</ul>`},{id:"saving",heading:"Saving and undo",html:`
<p>There's no Save button: a template saves itself a moment after each change, and when you close it. Undo with <kbd>Cmd</kbd> <kbd>Z</kbd> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Z</kbd> on Windows) and redo with <kbd>Cmd</kbd> <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>Z</kbd>. Every card made from the template, and every table showing them, updates at once.</p>`}],faq:[["Can I use different layouts for different kinds of cards?","Yes. Make a template for each kind, like creatures and spells, and pick each card’s template when you open it or when you import."],["Can cards be double-sided?","Yes. Set the template’s back to Double and give the back its own fields."],["Can I make landscape cards?","Yes. Set the template to Landscape in Settings. A landscape card takes the same space on the table as a portrait one, turned on its side."]]},{slug:"playtesting-with-others",nav:"Playtesting with others",title:"Playtesting with others",pageTitle:"Playtesting with others in Prototable: invites, seats and hands",description:"Invite players to your Prototable table with a link: Open, PIN or Approval, a seat and a hand each, or play every seat yourself with stand-ins.",lede:"Players join your table from a link in their browser. They need no account and nothing to install, and they play on the same table you set up.",updated:"2026-11-06",howTo:[["Sign in","A table you share needs an account. Sign in and your table gets a code."],["Open Host","Press Host and choose who can join: Open, PIN or Approval."],["Send the link","Press Copy the invite link and send it, or read out the code."],["Let them in","With Approval, press Approve on each player who asks to join."]],sections:[{id:"invite",heading:"Inviting players",html:`
<p>Sign in first: a table only gets a code you can share once it's saved to your account. Then press ${Dt("Share")} and choose who can join:</p>
<ul class="plain">
  <li><b>Open</b>: anyone with the link walks straight in.</li>
  <li><b>PIN</b>: they need the PIN as well as the link. A four-digit PIN is made for you, and you can change it.</li>
  <li><b>Approval</b>: they knock, and you let them in. ${Dt("Approve")} or ${Dt("Decline")} each one as they ask.</li>
</ul>
<p>Press ${Dt("Copy the invite link")}, or read out the code. Players open the link, type their name and press ${Dt("Join")}. Anyone with a code can also press ${Dt("Share")} and type it at the bottom.</p>`},{id:"how-many",heading:"How many can play",html:`
<p>A room holds ${cf("free")} players on the free plan, ${cf("paid1")} on Designer and ${cf("paid2")} on Studio. The limit comes from the plan of whoever owns the table, so your players never need a plan of their own. Only players who are online take a place, and stand-ins don't take one at all.</p>`},{id:"seats",heading:"Seats and hands",html:`
<p>Seats sit around the edge of the table, and each player who joins sits in the next free one. In ${Dt("Players")}, ${Dt("Sit here")} moves you to a seat and ${Dt("Stand")} leaves it. Only the host moves other players.</p>
<p>Each player has a hand for hidden cards. Everyone else sees only how many cards you hold.</p>`},{id:"stand-ins",heading:"Playing every seat yourself",html:`
<p>Before you invite anyone, play the game alone with stand-ins. In ${Dt("Players")}, under ${Dt("Play every seat yourself")}, press the + to add a stand-in in the next open seat. Each stand-in has its own hand and view.</p>
<p>Switch between them from the hand bar's ${Dt("Play as")} menu, or press <kbd>N</kbd> to play the next seat. ${Dt("Back to you")} returns you to your own seat.</p>`},{id:"at-the-table",heading:"At the table",html:`
<ul class="plain">
  <li>${Dt("Chat")} (<kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>C</kbd>) for messages, and ${Dt("Game log")} (<kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>G</kbd>) for what happened.</li>
  <li>Press <kbd>P</kbd> to ping the table.</li>
  <li>Edit a card mid-game and every copy changes at once, on the table, in hands and in decks.</li>
</ul>
<p>In ${Dt("Share")}, under ${Dt("Who’s here")}, the pencil lets a player edit the table's setup, and the remove button takes a player out (their hand goes back to the deck). Someone who leaves keeps their seat and hand for half an hour.</p>
<p>${Dt("Share")} also has ${Dt("Reset the table")}, ${Dt("Start a new game")} (which ends the session so the next one is recorded on its own), ${Dt("Clear chat")} and ${Dt("End the room")}.</p>`},{id:"public",heading:"Public games",html:`
<p>To let anyone play, ${Dt("Publish")} your game and set it to Public. It gets its own page that people find in Explore. A public room is for playing: nobody edits cards there, you included, unless you turn on ${Dt("Allow editing in public rooms")} under ${Dt("Publish")}, ${Dt("Public tables")}. Public playtests are recorded unless you turn off ${Dt("Record public playtests")}.</p>`},{id:"play-button",heading:"A Play button for your site",html:`
<p>Once your game is published, ${Dt("Publish")} shows ${Dt("Put a Play button on your site")} under its link. Pick ${Dt("Dark")} or ${Dt("Light")}, then copy it in the form your page takes: ${Dt("HTML")} for your own site, itch.io, WordPress, Squarespace or Carrd, ${Dt("Markdown")} for GitHub, Reddit or a blog, ${Dt("BBCode")} for BoardGameGeek, or ${Dt("Link")} for Kickstarter and anywhere code won&rsquo;t paste. Whoever clicks it lands on your game&rsquo;s page, a click away from a seat.</p>`}],faq:[["Do players need an account?","No. They join from your link in their browser, type a name, and play. Only the designer needs an account."],["Can people play on an iPad?","Yes. Prototable runs in the browser on a laptop, desktop, iPad or phone."],["What happens if someone drops out?","They keep their seat and hand for half an hour, so they can rejoin from the same link and carry on."],["Can I test a four-player game alone?","Yes. Add stand-ins to the empty seats and play each one yourself, switching with N."]]},{slug:"recording-and-review",nav:"Recording and review",title:"Recording and review",pageTitle:"Recording and reviewing playtests in Prototable",description:"Record a playtest in Prototable, leave notes on cards and collect feedback, then keep or discard each card change and restore past sessions.",lede:"A recorded playtest keeps the table as it started and ended, every card you changed, the notes, the chat, and what players said afterwards, so you can decide what to keep.",updated:"2026-11-03",howTo:[["Press Record","Press Record at the top right of your table, or Shift R. Add a question or two you want the playtest to answer, or record without goals."],["Play and take notes","Play as normal. Right-click a card and choose Add note to write down what you noticed."],["End the session","Press the timer to stop, say how the session felt, leave a wrap-up note, and end it."],["Review the changes","Open your game’s Playtests and keep or discard each card change."]],sections:[{id:"record",heading:"Recording a playtest",html:`
<p>Press ${Dt("Record")} at the top right of your table, or <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>R</kbd>. It first asks what this playtest should tell you: add a question or two that only you see, then ${Dt("Start recording")}, or choose ${Dt("Record without goals")}. The table owner or the acting host can start a recording.</p>
<p>A recording keeps the table as it was when it started, the game log, the chat, every card edited during the game, and the notes. A session nobody touches for two hours ends by itself.</p>`},{id:"notes",heading:"Notes on cards",html:`
<p>While recording, right-click a card and choose ${Dt("Add note")}. Tag it ${Dt("Balance")}, ${Dt("Idea")}, ${Dt("Feel")} or ${Dt("Bug")}, and write what you noticed. Everyone at the table sees notes, players can add their own, and they're kept with the playtest.</p>`},{id:"end",heading:"Ending a session",html:`
<p>Press the timer to stop. ${Dt("End session?")} shows how long it ran, the notes, the cards edited and the most discussed cards. Say how the session felt, from Bad to Great, leave a ${Dt("Wrap-up note")}, and press ${Dt("End session")}, or ${Dt("Keep playing")} to carry on.</p>
<p>When it ends, each player is asked how the game was, with a 1 to 5 scale and any questions you set under ${Dt("Publish")}, ${Dt("Player feedback")}. You can also ask a short mid-game question every few minutes.</p>`},{id:"review",heading:"Reviewing what changed",html:`
<p>Open your game's ${Dt("Playtests")} (a tab under its name), or ${Dt("Review")}, beside ${Dt("Record")} at your table. Every card edited during a recorded playtest waits under ${Dt("Card changes")}, before and after side by side:</p>
<ul class="plain">
  <li>${Dt("Keep")} makes the change stick.</li>
  <li>${Dt("Discard")} puts the card back as it was. In ${Dt("Review")} at your table, this is ${Dt("Put back")}.</li>
  <li>${Dt("Keep all")} and ${Dt("Discard all")} decide every change at once.</li>
</ul>
<p>How each card is playing shows how often it came into play, was drawn and was played, its notes, and its last edit. ${Dt("Players said")} collects the feedback. Both come with Designer. Pick a playtest for its recap, and write down what to try next time.</p>`},{id:"restore",heading:"Going back to a past session",html:`
<p>${Dt("Playtests")} lists every recording, each with its log and chat. ${Dt("Restore start")} or ${Dt("Restore end")} puts the table back how that session started or ended, and starts a new session from there, so nothing is lost. Your table must be open to restore.</p>`},{id:"limits",heading:"How many you can record",html:`
<p>The free plan records 3 playtests per game. Designer and Studio record as many as you like. At the limit, Record doesn't start a session and the game log says so. Public rooms record by themselves when someone joins, and those don't count toward it.</p>`}],faq:[["Does recording capture video?","No. It records the state of the table, the game log, chat, card edits and notes, which is what reviewing a playtest needs and takes far less space."],["Can I undo a card change made during a playtest?","Yes. In Playtests, under Card changes, Discard puts the card back exactly as it was before the session."],["Can players leave notes too?","Yes. Anyone at the table can right-click a card and add a note while a session is recording."]]},{slug:"plans",nav:"Plans",title:"Plans",pageTitle:"Prototable plans: Free, Designer and Studio compared",description:`What each Prototable plan includes: Free for ${gj()}, Designer for playtesting with your group, Studio for campaigns and teams. Players you invite always join free.`,lede:`Every plan is paid once a year, and players you invite always join free. The free plan is the whole table for ${gj()}.`,updated:"2026-11-03",sections:[{id:"compare",heading:"What each plan includes",html:`
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th>${Ff.map(e=>`<th scope="col">${qc(e.name)}</th>`).join("")}</tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><b>Price</b></td><td>$0</td><td>${qc(yA("designer"))}</td><td>${qc(yA("studio"))}</td></tr>
    ${Yb.map((e,t)=>{var s;return`${e.group!==((s=Yb[t-1])==null?void 0:s.group)?`<tr><th colspan="4" scope="colgroup">${qc(e.group)}</th></tr>
    `:""}<tr><td><b>${qc(e.label)}</b></td><td>${qc(e.free)}</td><td>${qc(e.designer)}</td><td>${qc(e.studio)}</td></tr>`}).join(`
    `)}
  </tbody>
</table></div>
${So()?"<p>Founding prices stay the same at renewal for as long as you keep your plan.</p>":""}`},{id:"plans",heading:"Which plan is for you",html:Ff.map(e=>`
<h3>${qc(e.name)}</h3>
<p>${qc(e.pitch)}</p>`).join("")+`
<p>Studio is for a game going in front of backers. Send us what you have and we import your game for you. Its collaborators are people you make editors of your game from your own table, each with the parts you choose. Your public game's page carries a badge linking to your Kickstarter or Gamefound campaign, your game is listed first in Explore, and its stats show plays, players and who came back.</p>`},{id:"limits",heading:"When you reach a limit",html:`
<ul class="plain">
  <li><b>Games</b>: the free plan keeps ${gj()}. Starting another asks you to choose a plan.</li>
  <li><b>Players</b>: a room seats ${cf("free")} on the free plan, ${cf("paid1")} on Designer and ${cf("paid2")} on Studio, set by whoever owns the table. Once it&rsquo;s full, the next person is told so.</li>
  <li><b>Recorded playtests</b>: after 3 on one free game, Record doesn't start and the game log says so. Your past sessions stay.</li>
  <li><b>Images</b>: the free plan imports 70 card pictures at a time and 200 images in all.</li>
</ul>`},{id:"buy",heading:"Upgrading",html:`
<p>Open ${Dt("Plans")} in the app and pick a plan. Payment is by card through Stripe, once a year. Designer starts with ${Ul} free days the first time you take it: cancel before they end and you aren&rsquo;t charged. See the <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a> for the same plans at a glance.</p>`}],faq:[["Do my players need a plan?","No. Players always join free. A room’s player limit comes from the plan of whoever owns the table."],["Is there a monthly plan?","No. Every plan is paid once a year."],["What does the free plan include?","Two games on the full table, 2 players a room, 3 recorded playtests, 200 image uploads, and per game 5 card templates, 2 board templates, 5 token templates, 2 custom dice, 10 macros and 10 automations, printing with a watermark."]]}],MF=["new","overview","publish","data","boards","tokens","dice","templates","boardtemplates","tokentemplates","dicetemplates","decks","import","print","rules","settings","turns","ideas","recordings","playtests","changes","cardstats","said","notes","account","achievements","collaborators","connect","pieces","designs","sounds","pricing"],l3e=["/","/explore","/browse","/login","/signup","/app","/home","/home/:page","/home/help/:slug","/help","/help/:slug","/games","/admin","/room/:roomId","/join/:roomId","/join","/play/:roomCode","/set/:setId","/game/:slug","/games/:gameId","/privacy","/terms","/snap",...MF.map(e=>`/${e}`)];l3e.map(e=>new RegExp(`^${e==="/"?"":e.replace(/:\w+/g,"[^/]+")}/?$`,"i"));const Oie=e=>typeof Intl<"u"&&typeof Intl.Segmenter=="function"?new Intl.Segmenter(void 0,{granularity:e}):null,KG=Oie("word"),XG=Oie("grapheme"),c3e=/^[\p{Ps}\p{Pi}]+$/u,d3e=/^\s+$/;function u3e(e){const t=KG?Array.from(KG.segment(e),r=>({text:r.segment,word:!!r.isWordLike})):e.split(/(\s+)/).filter(Boolean).map(r=>({text:r,word:!d3e.test(r)})),s=[];let a="";for(const{text:r,word:i}of t)i?(s.push(a+r),a=""):c3e.test(r)?a+=r:s.length&&!a?s[s.length-1]+=r:a+=r;return a&&s.push(a),s}function h3e(e){return XG?Array.from