they leave",body:"Players now get asked how your game was when they press Leave, even if you weren't recording. One tap on a face and they're on their way, or they can just leave. Their answers land in Review with the rest of your player feedback."},{date:"2026-11-05",tag:"improvement",title:"A calmer header at other tables",body:"At a table you're visiting, Leave now sits right beside the table's name, and the room's code and player count are in that name's menu. The rest of the header is just who's playing and Share, so Table and Explore stay in the middle."},{date:"2026-11-05",tag:"improvement",title:"Shared tables get the bigger table",body:"Tables you join by link, and classic tables when you're signed out, now use the same top bar as your own table, so the sidebar is gone and the table has more room. Explore in the header works from these tables too, and the player list no longer covers it on a smaller window."},{date:"2026-11-05",tag:"improvement",title:"A background for your macro list",body:"Macro and automation names sit right on the table, which gets hard to read once there are cards under them. Flip the new switch beside the heading to put a solid background behind the list. It stays how you left it on this device. Long lists now scroll too, instead of squashing their names together."},{date:"2026-11-05",tag:"feature",title:"Free printable card and board templates",body:"Trading card, playing card, card game and board game templates now come as free PDFs, with no sign-up and no watermark. Print a sheet and sketch your first version by hand, then bring it to a table when you're ready."},{date:"2026-11-04",tag:"feature",title:"Hearts is on the classic tables",body:"Sit down with three or four friends and the table deals, tells you which way to pass each hand, says when hearts are broken, and adds up the points as tricks are taken. It's also a good one to borrow from if you're designing a trick-taking game."},{date:"2026-11-04",tag:"feature",title:"Send us an idea from the table",body:"There's a new lightbulb in the bottom right of the table, and Send feedback in Help. Tell Jeremy about an idea or something that's missing without leaving your game, signed in or not."},{date:"2026-11-04",tag:"improvement",title:"A calmer header",body:"Table and Explore now sit on their own in the middle of the header, with Publish and a new Share button on the right. Share holds everything Host did, plus a box for joining a table with a code."},{date:"2026-11-02",tag:"fix",title:"Deleting a template keeps its cards looking right",body:"Cards on a template you delete now move to your game's default template, instead of an old layout that hid most of what you'd written."},{date:"2026-11-02",tag:"fix",title:"Importing a spreadsheet keeps your template",body:"A column like Rarity could be matched to Template, which turned your cards into an old layout with most of their text missing. Only a column that really names your templates can choose one now, and what the preview shows is what you get."},{date:"2026-11-02",tag:"feature",title:"Put your table somewhere",body:"The table no longer has to float in the dark. Grid and table now has backdrops: real places like a gothic cloister, a castle dungeon, a cave, a mossy forest, or a beach, plus a plain studio and space. The photo carries on under the table as a real floor, so turn on Table legs and it stands there. Both are just your view, so everyone at the table can pick their own."},{date:"2026-11-02",tag:"fix",title:"Play counts now count real games",body:"A table only counts as played once people actually play at it, not just because it was opened. The number on your game's page and in your stats may drop, but now it means something."},{date:"2026-11-02",tag:"fix",title:"Wide cards show the right way up on game pages",body:"Landscape cards were drawn sideways and cut off on a game’s page. They now show upright and whole, and a game with both wide and tall cards keeps its rows even."},{date:"2026-11-02",tag:"feature",title:"A new Publish, and stats for your game",body:"Publish is now your game's page itself: press what you want to change, see what's still missing beside it, and publish from the button at the top or from Publish at the table. A game listed for everyone stays up for 15 days, so people who find it can come back. Public tables sets how the tables strangers open start, including which layout they open with, and Player feedback (Designer) lets you start from a ready-made form and change what players are asked, right on it. A new Stats tab counts your page views and tables played on every plan, and Studio adds who sat down, who came back, and where they found you."},{date:"2026-11-02",tag:"improvement",title:"Game pages show one thing at a time",body:"How to play, The cards and About the designer are real tabs now, and every card is there to look through, large. Just me, With friends and With anyone are right at the top of the page, with more games to play at the bottom."},{date:"2026-11-02",tag:"feature",title:"Make takes over from the sidebar",body:"The sidebar is gone, so the table has the whole window. What it held is now in Make, the first button at the table’s top left, or press M: your cards and templates, decks, rules, sounds, import and print, with the table still in view beside it. Pick a card and press M to open Make on that card, or grow Make wide when a page needs the room. The header now reads Table | Explore, so your table is always one press away."},{date:"2026-11-02",tag:"improvement",title:"Free holds 2 games, and Designer seats 6",body:"Free now holds 2 games instead of 1, so you can start something new without giving up the last one. Designer tables now seat up to 6, a whole playtest group, and 12 seats moves to Studio for campaign demos and teams. If you bought Designer for a bigger table, tell me on Discord and we’ll sort it out."},{date:"2026-11-02",tag:"improvement",title:"The table is much easier on a phone",body:"On a phone the table now opens flat and close on what’s in play, so piles and cards are big enough to read, held upright or sideways. The bars are gone: tap the game’s name for one menu with everything in it, Make included. Prefer the 3D view? It’s in that menu, and your phone remembers."},{date:"2026-11-02",tag:"feature",title:"A new dark look, and a choice of three",body:"Dark mode is now Charcoal, a plain neutral gray around the table. Prefer the old look? Settings → Appearance has a Color: Charcoal, Slate (what it was), or Midnight. Your felt and cards stay as they are."},{date:"2026-11-02",tag:"fix",title:"Undo and Pass turn keys work after you click the table",body:"Once you’d clicked the felt, Cmd+Z, Space and Esc could stop doing anything until you clicked somewhere else. They now work wherever you last clicked."},{date:"2026-11-02",tag:"improvement",title:"Learn walks you through Make",body:"The lessons in Learn now follow Make and the table as they are today, and you can start any of them, or start one again, whenever you like. The tutorial sits at the top of Learn too, for when you want to go back to the beginning."},{date:"2026-11-02",tag:"improvement",title:"Every tab in Make has its own key",body:"Option and a number jump to a tab in Make, in the order they run down its rail: Option+4 for Data, Option+7 for Rules. Press it again to close. The old ⇧D, ⇧Y, ⇧I, ⇧X and ⇧Z now open their tabs in Make too, and you can change any of them in Settings."},{date:"2026-11-01",tag:"fix",title:"The table runs cool while you think",body:"A few small shimmers and pulsing dots, like the Upgrade sparkle and the recording dot, looped forever, which kept your browser redrawing the whole table even when nothing moved. They now play a few times and settle, so an idle table no longer spins up your fans or drains your battery. The bars that sit on the table are solid charcoal now instead of frosted glass, which halves the work of every move, drag and zoom."},{date:"2026-11-01",tag:"improvement",title:"Review your playtests beside the table",body:"Review, beside Record, now holds everything Playtest review did. Keep or put back each card change right on its row, or open one to see the card before and after with the changed words marked. Open a playtest for its recap, and bring the table back to how it started or ended. Your notes are now filed under the playtest they were written in, with new ones kept for the next playtest."},{date:"2026-10-26",tag:"feature",title:"Import a sheet of tokens",body:"Tokens → Import a sheet takes a PDF or image with your tokens printed on it, finds every piece, and makes each one a token with its own art and color, all from one template. Name the rows and columns (Red, Blue… and Burst, Wave…) to name them all at once, and pieces from a PDF come out at their printed size, beside your cards. In Add, your tokens now show as themselves, and All tokens puts one of each on the table at once, laid out in a grid."},{date:"2026-10-26",tag:"feature",title:"Turn a pile to face its owner",body:"A pile’s Facing settings now have Points to: Its owner turns the pile’s cards toward the seat it belongs to, so in a two-player game each deck and discard reads the right way up for its player. You can also point a pile at any side of the table, or press Q or E over it to turn it, and its outline turns with it. If an outline ends up the wrong way round, Fit the pile to its cards puts it right."},{date:"2026-10-26",tag:"fix",title:"Picking a back no longer adds a template",body:"When you picked the backs in a PDF import, each page you used as a back made a template of its own, and the first template was left with no back. Now your first back goes on that first template, and only another back makes another one."},{date:"2026-10-26",tag:"fix",title:"Removing a pile takes its cards with it",body:"Removing a pile used to leave all its cards behind in a stack where it stood. Now the cards you chose for it leave the table with it (they’re still in your game), and only a card someone put there during play is left on the table. Undo brings the pile back with its cards."},{date:"2026-10-26",tag:"improvement",title:"A PDF import makes a deck",body:"Cards brought in from a PDF now also go in a deck named after their template, one deck for each template, so a pile can start with them by picking that deck instead of ticking every card. A pile’s Specific cards list also shows newly imported cards straight away, without a reload."},{date:"2026-10-25",tag:"improvement",title:"Help on the left",body:"Help now sits at the bottom of the tabs on the left: interactive lessons that run on your table, the documentation (now a window you can read beside your game), the keyboard shortcuts, the Discord, and bug reports, all in one place. Publish moved off that list, since it’s already in the top bar, and What’s new is now the megaphone just above your picture."},{date:"2026-10-25",tag:"improvement",title:"Import has its own menu",body:"Import on the left now opens a menu like Cards does: pick what you have (a Tabletop Simulator deck, a spreadsheet, pictures, a PDF) and its importer opens with the steps beside it. Drop a file still works out what you have for you. Sounds opens a menu the same way now, with a row for each part."},{date:"2026-10-25",tag:"improvement",title:"Drag pieces out of Add",body:"Press a piece, die, counter, deck or card in Add and drag it onto the table: it lands where you let go, instead of in the middle of the view. A click still puts it down as before, and the tray stays open after a drag so you can bring in the next. All now shows everything at once, each with a picture of what you get, playing cards and the chess set included. The pawn is ivory now, to match the chess pieces beside it."},{date:"2026-10-25",tag:"improvement",title:"Chess pieces and the rulebook in Add",body:"Add → Pieces now has a king, queen, rook, bishop and knight beside the pawn, so you can put down one chess piece without the whole set, and the rulebook sits next to the bag."},{date:"2026-10-24",tag:"improvement",title:"Turn your phone sideways to play",body:"On a phone, the table is now made to be played sideways: the whole table fits, the right way up, with the same rail, panels and buttons as on a computer, just smaller. Holding it upright at a table asks you to turn it, and Stay upright keeps the upright table if you’d rather. Everything else, from your games to Explore, stays upright."},{date:"2026-10-24",tag:"fix",title:"A classic opened on a phone opens its table",body:"On a first visit from a phone, picking a classic like Blackjack and choosing Just me left you on the home screen instead of at the table. It opens the table now, and reloading a table you sat at puts you back in your seat without asking your name again."},{date:"2026-10-24",tag:"improvement",title:"A phone held upright shows your end of the table",body:"On a phone held upright, the table no longer turns sideways to fill the screen, which left every card reading the wrong way. It shows your end of the table the right way up, and the far ends run off the sides: swipe to look along them. A little map under the view controls shows the whole table with what you can see outlined. Tap it for the whole table, and tap again to come back."},{date:"2026-10-24",tag:"improvement",title:"Publish is in the header",body:"Publish sits at the top of your table now, beside What’s new, and reads Live once others can play. Click it to see where your game stands and copy its link, then go on to the full Publish page to change its details or who can play."},{date:"2026-10-24",tag:"improvement",title:"Templates are called Templates again",body:"The layout a kind of card, board or token is made from is a template again, not a design: the Templates page, New template and Edit template. Nothing about how they work has changed."},{date:"2026-10-24",tag:"improvement",title:"Several templates from one PDF, each with its own back",body:`PDF import now asks where your backs are before anything comes in, and shows every card with the back it will have.
- Front, back, front, back: each card gets the page printed after it. Cards with the same back share a template, and you can swap a card’s sides or skip it.
- I’ll pick the backs: select pages, then give them a back from the bar at the bottom, use them as a back, or skip them. Cards that have their back grey out, so the ones still waiting stand out.
- The card size is read from your PDF’s pages, bleed and all, and you can change it in the sidebar beside Add more PDFs.
- Import takes you straight to the new cards in Cards.`},{date:"2026-10-24",tag:"improvement",title:"Put a rulebook down in Playtest, and lock it",body:"A rulebook is added like a bag now, from Add or Shift+F while you play, instead of only in Edit. Drag it where you want it, press + or − over it to resize it, and Lock it so nobody knocks it aside: a locked book still opens and turns its pages. Its title, look and color are in its panel when you select it."},{date:"2026-10-24",tag:"fix",title:"Rulebook pages stay behind what’s in front of them",body:"An open rulebook’s pages used to draw over everything, so a bag or a pawn standing in front of the book seemed to sit behind it, and a piece set on the pages vanished under them. Now whatever is nearer covers the pages. Reset size also works on tokens and bags resized before it existed: it puts them back to their usual size."},{date:"2026-10-24",tag:"improvement",title:"A real drawstring bag, with your own print",body:`Bags are bigger now, a soft velvet pouch with a cord tied round the neck, and they open when you point at them or something goes in or comes out. A piece drawn out pops up from the mouth and lands where it goes, and one put back drops in.
- Upload a picture under Bag picture and it’s printed on both sides. A maker’s proof on a white sheet is cut down to the bag, and the cloth takes its color.
- Lock a bag and it stays put: drag off it to pull a piece out at random, right onto your pointer, the way you take a card off a pile. Double-click still draws one beside it.
- Select several pieces and drag them onto a bag, or press Put in bag, to put them all in at once.
- Dice go in bags too, and a die drawn out comes out rolled.`},{date:"2026-10-24",tag:"improvement",title:"A wooden table, and the grid a click away",body:"The table can be bare wood now instead of felt: oak, walnut, cherry, or any color you pick, like the kitchen table a prototype starts on. Pick one under Grid, beside Macros and Automations at the bottom left, where showing, snapping and sizing the grid now live too. Both are just for your screen."},{date:"2026-10-24",tag:"feature",title:"Hold Option to look up close",body:`Hold Option (Alt on Windows) over anything on the table and it comes up big in the middle of the screen until you let go: a card, the top of a pile, a meeple, a die, a counter, or the picture on a board or mat. Move the pointer while you hold it to look at the next thing. Over a rulebook it opens the pages.
- Point at a piece and press + or − to make it bigger or smaller, for everyone at the table. Over anything else they still zoom. Reset size in its panel puts it back.
- Rebind either in Settings → Shortcuts, Look up close to ⌘ if you prefer.`},{date:"2026-10-24",tag:"feature",title:"Bring your rulebook in as a PDF",body:"Rules → Import a PDF turns a rulebook into a tab of its pages, each one exactly as you laid it out. Players read it a page at a time with Back and Next, zoom in or go full screen for small print, and search marks the word right on its page. On the table the book takes the shape of your PDF’s pages, so each one fills its page, and the full-screen book zooms too. Scans or photos of pages come in the same way."},{date:"2026-10-22",tag:"improvement",title:"A quieter top on your game’s pages",body:"Explore and What’s new no longer repeat at the top of your game’s pages: Explore sits beside New game in the sidebar, and What’s new is in your menu at its foot. Settings has a row in the sidebar now too."},{date:"2026-10-22",tag:"fix",title:"Playing cards and chess are back in Add",body:"Add → Decks has a deck of playing cards again, beside your own decks: 52 cards and two jokers, shuffled, as a pile. Add → Boards has a chess set, a board with all 32 pieces set up. Search finds both."},{date:"2026-10-22",tag:"improvement",title:"Learn is back, with tours for making cards, tokens, boards and dice",body:`Learn sits beside Host and Join again. Four new tours walk you through designing a card, a token, a board, and a die of your own, from a blank design to one on the table.
- Every tour now follows the tabs down the left and the Add menu, so each step points at the button you need, even inside a menu that’s closed.`},{date:"2026-10-22",tag:"improvement",title:"Your actions ride under the turn bar",body:`A phase's steps now sit in a strip attached under the turn bar, filling in as the phase goes on, instead of a second bar floating over the table.
- On your go, the step tucks into a tag and your actions slide into that same strip. Point at one to see what it does.
- With more actions than fit, they show as icons, each named when you point at it.
- The full menu that dropped over the table is gone.`},{date:"2026-10-22",tag:"improvement",title:"Steps that play their own way, and a smaller action menu",body:`The steps of a phase can now each be played differently.
- An action phase can have a Start and End the table resolves, with players taking turns in between. Set it under each step, in Played by.
- The action menu only shows on the steps players play, and a phase can have both steps and actions.
- The menu now sits under the turn bar as one row of names, so it no longer covers the table. Hover an action for its reminder, or open the arrow for the full list.`},{date:"2026-10-22",tag:"improvement",title:"Layouts you can see",body:`Layouts now has its own spot in the rail, with your layouts and the examples on tabs, and every layout shows a picture of its table: its mats, piles and seats. Table has its own tab above it too, for the players, the score, what’s on the table and starting over.
- See what’s on the table now and save it as a layout, or save over the one you’re using.
- Click a saved layout’s picture to set the table up that way, and mark the one public games and new rooms open with.
- Presets show their tables too, and now set how many seats the table has.`},{date:"2026-10-22",tag:"improvement",title:"Sit down with one click",body:`Views has joined the rail beside Layouts, and the View button opens it too: the table from above with every seat and camera, numbered like the keys that jump to them, with 2D or 3D, turning the table, grid and felt, and seeing it as a player all in one place.
- Once someone else is at your table, every empty seat shows a plus on the table’s edge. Click it to sit there, and your hand and view move with you. Choose in Views whether the pluses show with others, always or never.
- While you edit the table, Seats shows how many chairs it has and who sits where. The button that used to say Seat made a camera, so it’s called Camera now.`},{date:"2026-10-22",tag:"improvement",title:"Close eyes hides the whole table",body:"Table → Close eyes now opens as a window you can move, and the table is hidden completely for everyone asleep, yours too unless you pick yourself to stay awake. Choose whether their hand hides as well (it does by default). The rest of the screen stays, so the host opens everyone’s eyes from the dark table, and it fades back in as they open."},{date:"2026-10-22",tag:"improvement",title:"Claude and ChatGPT can set up your turn bar",body:"A connected app can now read and set up your game's turn bar: its phases, who plays each, the steps inside a phase, the actions players pick from, and where each round starts. Ask it to lay out your turn from your rules, and it can add the automations and macros that go with each phase too."},{date:"2026-10-21",tag:"improvement",title:"A calmer bar while you edit the table",body:`Editing the table now shows Done editing on its own and one strip of tools.
- Turns leads the strip and opens your turn bar setup in a sidebar on the right, with each phase and action opening inside it, and the bar shown on the table beside it.
- Add, Sound and Record step aside while you edit (⇧A still opens Add).
- In a narrower window the tools show as icons, and narrower still, Heading, Text, Rulebook, Pile, Lane, Mat and Seat fold into one Lay out menu, so nothing covers Done editing.`},{date:"2026-10-21",tag:"improvement",title:"Editor panels move like windows",body:"Add a step, a rule’s When, and the Turn bar’s phase and action pages now drag by their header and resize from their corner, like every other window. The next one opens where you left the last, and a double-click on the header docks it back beside its panel."},{date:"2026-10-21",tag:"feature",title:"Actions to pick from on your go",body:`A phase can now list what a player may do on their go, like Play a card, Attack or Take the initiative, and they pick one from a menu under the turn bar.
- Each action has a reminder line, so players don’t need the rulebook open to know their choices.
- Picking one ends your go, counts as passing, or lets you keep going, as you set it, and everyone sees what you picked.
- An action can run a macro, like taking the first-player marker.
- Rule an action out when the marker was already taken this round, your hand is empty, a pile is empty or a counter is low. It shows dimmed, with why.
- Pass is always in the menu. Set up actions in the Turn bar, under each phase.`},{date:"2026-10-21",tag:"feature",title:"Phases with steps in the turn bar",body:`A turn or a round can now be phases that hold steps, for games with more structure than one list.
- Group steps into a phase (Regroup: Draw, Resource, Ready). The bar shows the phases, and the one you’re in shows its steps in a row under it.
- Say who plays each phase: whoever’s turn it is, each player in turn, everyone together, the table, or players taking turns until everyone passes, with a Pass button on the bar.
- Start each round from the first seat, the next seat round, or whoever holds a first-player marker you name, like Initiative or Start player.
- Give a phase a short note shown under the bar, like what a player can do.
- Macros can pass for the player whose go it is, and hand the first-player marker over.`},{date:"2026-10-21",tag:"improvement",title:"Icons for your turn steps",body:"Each step in your turn bar can now carry an icon beside its name, like swords for a fight or coins for buying, so players see where the turn is at a glance. Pick one with the button beside the step in the Turn bar setup. Steps without one look just as they did, and automations still find steps by name."},{date:"2026-10-21",tag:"improvement",title:"Steps and conditions by category",body:`Picking a step or a condition is quicker.
- Add a step shows every step as an icon under its category (Cards, Counters, Pieces, Words, Turns, Hidden roles), with search, and says what the one you point at does.
- An automation’s When is a card: press it to change its pile or number, or Change to pick a different condition from the same kind of list.
- A macro or automation that names a pile, counter or bag no longer on the table is marked in amber, so you know it won’t run or which step it skips.`},{date:"2026-10-21",tag:"feature",title:"Macros for bags, dice, tokens and the rulebook",body:`Macros and automations now reach every piece on the table.
- Bags: draw in front of whoever’s turn it is or each player, put the drawn pieces back, or empty the bag onto the table.
- Dice: set a die to a face, or run an automation when a die rolls a 6 or the dice total 10 or more.
- Tokens and marbles: move one onto a mat or a square of its grid, knock pieces over or stand them up, or run an automation when a red cube is drawn or a piece lands on a mat.
- Rulebook: open it at a section or page, or close it.`},{date:"2026-10-21",tag:"improvement",title:"Your game first in the sidebar",body:"The sidebar now opens on your game: New game and Explore sit side by side at the top, then your game and its parts, then Table and Join a table. Explore is also in the top bar on your game’s pages, and first in the rail beside the table, so other designers’ games are a click away wherever you are."},{date:"2026-10-21",tag:"improvement",title:"The sidebar matches the table",body:"On every page, the sidebar now lists your game’s parts in the same order and colors as the rail beside the table, so Cards or Playtests is one click from anywhere. Click the game’s name to switch to another of your games or start a new one. Join a table moved from the top bar to the sidebar, signed in or not: it takes a code, shows whose table it is before you go in, and lists the tables you’ve joined before."},{date:"2026-10-21",tag:"improvement",title:"Every piece on the free plan",body:"Health bars, grids on mats, custom dice and your own token designs are on every plan again. The free plan makes up to 5 card designs, 2 board designs, 5 token designs and 2 custom dice a game, and the ones you have keep working. Designer has as many as you like."},{date:"2026-10-21",tag:"improvement",title:"Ten macros a game on the free plan",body:"The free plan now has 10 macros a game, like its 10 automations, and every macro you already have keeps working. Designer has as many of both as you like."},{date:"2026-10-20",tag:"feature",title:"Kickstarter and Gamefound badges",body:"On Studio, paste your campaign’s link in Publish, beside your tagline, and your game’s page and its card in Browse carry a Back it on Kickstarter or Gamefound badge. Studio games are also listed first in Browse, and Studio now includes us importing your game for you."},{date:"2026-10-20",tag:"improvement",title:"Plans that say what you get",body:"The Plans window now says who each plan is for and what it adds, with every limit in Compare plans underneath. Secret votes and new health bars are part of Designer now; bars already on your table keep working."},{date:"2026-10-20",tag:"fix",title:"Translated pages no longer crash",body:"With your browser translating Prototable into another language, the app could crash to its error screen as the page changed. It now carries on, and the translation keeps working."},{date:"2026-10-20",tag:"improvement",title:"Your game’s numbers, on its Overview",body:"Every game’s Overview now opens with its numbers from all your playtests, public or not: how many, how long you’ve played, how many different players, how many cards you changed while playing, and playtests a week over the last twelve weeks."},{date:"2026-10-20",tag:"improvement",title:"Your first game, step by step, with achievements",body:"The Overview’s checklist now goes in order, and “Set up your table” is now “Make the table yours”, done when you change the table yourself rather than when it’s laid out for you. Each step earns an achievement, and every achievement now arrives as a banner with a burst of confetti. A new game’s Overview also shows the numbers its Publish page will track, waiting for its first play."},{date:"2026-10-20",tag:"improvement",title:"Option+1 to 8 open your game’s tabs",body:"Each tab down the sidebar now has a key by its place: Option+1 for Overview through Option+8 for Publish. At the table it opens the tab’s panel, on your game’s pages it opens the page, and each tab’s tooltip shows its key. B and R still open Cards and Playtests."},{date:"2026-10-20",tag:"improvement",title:"What’s new, next to Join",body:"Prototable changes most days, so What’s new now sits in the header beside Host and Join, on the table and on your game’s pages, with a dot when there’s something you haven’t read. It lists each change on one line to scan, opens one to read it, and Read all updates shows every change in full."},{date:"2026-10-20",tag:"improvement",title:"Vote for every seat you play",body:"Playing several seats yourself, a vote now gives you a ballot for each of them, with a chip to switch between seats, so you can test a vote alone. Before, your stand-ins counted as voters nobody could vote for, and the vote waited on them."},{date:"2026-10-20",tag:"fix",title:"A vote with nobody to pick no longer opens",body:"A Vote step for other players skips itself when a voter has nobody but themselves to pick, such as when you test alone, instead of opening an empty vote and holding up the rest of the macro."},{date:"2026-10-20",tag:"feature",title:"Votes at the table",body:"A new Vote step in macros asks everyone a question they answer by picking a player. Nobody sees a pick until the last one is in, then the whole table sees who pointed at whom, and the steps after it can name the winner as {{chosen}}. The host can reveal early or call a vote again."},{date:"2026-10-20",tag:"feature",title:"Narrate your game, line by line",body:"Game sounds can now run up to a minute. A Play sound step can show its words on every screen as it plays, eyes closed or not, and wait for the sound to end, so a macro can read out a night one role at a time. Close eyes can also hold the macro until eyes open, and Wait steps now go up to ten minutes. A running macro can be stopped from its message at the bottom right."},{date:"2026-10-20",tag:"fix",title:"One-night games wake the right player",body:"Close eyes can wake whoever held a card when the macro started, so a swap earlier in the night no longer wakes the wrong person. A role nobody holds still gets its turn with nobody awake, so the table can’t tell it’s in the middle."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Import is a tab in your game",body:"Bringing in a TTS deck, a spreadsheet or card pictures now lives in your game, as the tab right after Overview, so it always imports into the game you’re looking at. Integrations moved down beside Help in the sidebar."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Recall sends the cards flying home",body:"Press R on a pile, empty or not, and its cards lift off the table and out of everyone’s hands and arc back onto it one after another, instead of jumping there all at once."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"fix",title:"Cards face whoever puts them down",body:"A card dragged out of your hand or off a deck now lands the right way up for you, whichever side of the table you’re looking from. Sitting across the table, or with the view turned round, cards used to come out upside down."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"feature",title:"Import cards from PDFs",body:"Drop the PDFs you made for print on Import, from Illustrator, InDesign or a print-and-play file, and each page becomes a card. Drop a whole folder of one-card PDFs and they all come in together, on a card design you pick, with the back you upload for it. A page with a grid of cards on it is cut into its cards instead."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"improvement",title:"The same sidebar, signed in or not",body:"Before you sign up, the sidebar beside your table now has the same tabs as everyone else’s: Overview, Cards, Pieces, Decks, Rules, Playtests and Sounds. What needs a free account shows a lock and says what it adds. At someone else’s table, the sidebar keeps to what a player needs: the rules and the sounds."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"feature",title:"Your own sounds at the table",body:"On Designer, add your own clips on a game’s new Sounds tab: a creak recorded on your stairs, the narrator’s line, up to 10 seconds each. Press one at the table under Sounds → Custom and everyone hears it, and macros, Close eyes and the table’s own triggers can play them too. What the table plays by itself is now set on the game, so every table of it sounds the same, public ones included."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Sounds in the sidebar",body:"Sounds now has its own spot in the sidebar beside the table, just above Publish. It opens one window with everything about sound: play a knock for everyone, pick and preview what the table plays by itself, and a This device tab for your own volume and mute."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Real table sounds",body:"Dice, shuffles, cards, chips, knocks and footsteps are now recordings of the real thing instead of sounds made up in the browser, with a few takes of each so the same card sound twice in a row isn’t identical. The dice sound as they leave your hand, not after they land."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"feature",title:"Every feature, on one page",body:"prototable.gg/features lists everything Prototable does, grouped the way you meet it: card designs and imports, the 3D table, playing together, sounds, macros, recorded playtests, review, collaborators and printing. Send it to the co-designer who asks what it can do."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"improvement",title:"A week left for founding prices",body:"Founding prices end on October 26, so a strip across the top of the app now counts down to it, with Claim offer to open the plans: Designer $39 a year, Studio $79, kept for as long as you keep your plan. Close it with the X and it stays away until the last two days."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"fix",title:"Double-click a deck as fast as you like",body:"Double-clicking a pile to draw used to lift its top card on every click, so quick double-clicks lagged behind the animation, and partway down a deck they only selected it. Now a click never lifts the card, a double-click anywhere on the pile draws, and only a drag picks the card up."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"feature",title:"Eyes closed, and sounds at the table",body:"For hidden-role games: close everyone’s eyes but the players you name, and only the table goes dark for them. A Close eyes step in a macro can wake whoever holds a card, ask them to choose a player, and end on its own. Sound sits by Record: play a knock or footsteps for everyone, and the table now clatters dice, riffles shuffles and chimes as the turn passes."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"fix",title:"Minimize puts a window beside chat",body:"A minimized window used to vanish with no way back but opening it again. It now shrinks into a chip at the bottom right, beside Chat and Log, and a click on the chip brings it back as you left it."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"improvement",title:"A Settings tab for each game",body:"A game’s name, players and length, color, collaborators, and download, archive or delete now live together in Settings, beside Playtests. Publish is just about the page people see, and Host is just about inviting."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Game pages, full width",body:"A game’s page now opens on a hero across the whole page, its art behind a big title, with How to play and Play on its edge. Play opens the three ways to sit down: alone, with friends, or with anyone."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"feature",title:"Popular, in the sidebar",body:"Above your games, Popular shows the three games people sit down at most, with how many are playing right now. For now that’s the classics; as designers publish theirs, the most played rise to the top. Fold it away with its heading, or See all for Explore."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"improvement",title:"You land on your game",body:"Signed in, Prototable now opens on the game you were working on, at its Overview, instead of a Home page that repeated the sidebar. H takes you to Explore, where other designers’ games are, and the logo brings you back to your game."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Explore, and every game in the sidebar",body:"Browse is now Explore, at prototable.gg/explore (old /browse links still work): classic tables, open tables and games designers have published. The sidebar lists every one of your games, starred first, in a list that scrolls under its heading, and starts with one New game button. Import sits under Explore, Help sits above you, and light or dark mode is next to the logo."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Integrations has its own page",body:"Connect apps is now Integrations, a page of its own in the sidebar rather than a tab of Account. It opens with what you can ask a connected app to do, then Claude and ChatGPT by their logos with one button each, and Google Sheets to bring your cards in. Deleting your account now sits in a Danger zone and asks you to type your email first."},{date:"2026-10-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Turns are a list of steps again",body:"Turns now shows the bar it makes, across the top of the table, and each starting point is the bar it would give you. Steps are plain names again: Next walks through them, past the last one the turn passes, and automations and macros fire on them as before. A game that already marks steps for each player or everyone keeps working, with a button to make them plain."},{date:"2026-10-18",tag:"improvement",title:"A simpler sidebar, and one row of tabs for each game",body:"The sidebar now lists your games, with New game and Import cards at the top. Pick one and everything in it is one row of tabs: Overview, Cards, Pieces, Decks, Rules, Playtests and Publish, with how far the game has come beside its name. Card designs sit beside your cards now, a column that picks one and edits it. A game’s Overview is a checklist from first card to first playtest with others, with your own cards on it, and a picture of your table at the bottom right of every page takes you back to it. At the table, the sidebar is those same tabs, each opening its tools over the table, and Host and Join are on every page too. Rules are written right on the page, beside the rulebook players read, and Publish sits by Open table, one part at a time. A new game now opens on its first card, or its Overview, rather than an empty table."},{date:"2026-10-17",tag:"improvement",title:"Pieces sway as you carry them",body:"Pawns, meeples, dice, tokens and counters now lean the way you’re moving them, more the faster you go, swing back upright when you stop, and rock a little as you set them down. Only you see it, so nothing changes for anyone else at the table, and it stays off if your device is set to reduce motion."},{date:"2026-10-16",tag:"feature",title:"Try Designer free for a week",body:"When Free’s one game is full and you want another, you can now try Designer free for 7 days instead of buying it outright. Start before October 26 and the founding price, $39 a year, is yours for as long as you keep the plan. Cancel before the week ends and you pay nothing."},{date:"2026-10-16",tag:"improvement",title:"A quicker start on a new game",body:"A new game’s empty table now asks where your cards are first, with the four ways in at the top. Closed it by accident? A Start your game button stays on the empty table to bring it back."},{date:"2026-10-16",tag:"feature",title:"Add collaborators by email, and choose what each can change",body:"On Studio, a game’s Collaborators page lists everyone on it, and you can add anyone by their email: the next time they sign in to Prototable with it, the game is in their list. No email is sent, so tell them yourself. Each person has a switch for cards, designs, boards and pieces, decks, rules, the table and Review: everything is on to start, and with everything off they can still open the game and play."},{date:"2026-10-15",tag:"feature",title:"Collaborators: design a game together",body:"On Studio, open Host at your table and press Make collaborator beside anyone there who’s signed in. The game goes into their game list, and they can edit its cards, card designs, decks, rules and table whenever they like, with or without you there. Every game has a Collaborators place in the sidebar: who’s on it, how to add someone, and a way to take them off or to leave a game shared with you."},{date:"2026-10-15",tag:"feature",title:"Stats for your public game",body:"On Studio, your game’s Publish page shows how it does at its public tables: plays all time and in the last 30 days, how many different people played, how many came back on another day, plays week by week, and the latest plays with how many sat down."},{date:"2026-10-14",tag:"feature",title:"Import: pick one, and we walk you through it",body:"Import has its own place in the sidebar, and opens on a few plain choices: a Tabletop Simulator deck, a spreadsheet, picture files, or nothing yet. Pick one and a guide walks you through getting your file in. Already have it? Drop it or paste a link anywhere on the page, and Prototable works out what it is."},{date:"2026-10-13",tag:"improvement",title:"A clearer screen for a sleeping table",body:"When a table has been put away after a quiet stretch, it now says which game it is and what waking it brings back, with one button to wake it. It also stays inside the app’s frame instead of turning the whole window black."},{date:"2026-10-11",tag:"improvement",title:"Every new game walks you through bringing cards in",body:"Starting a new game from a Tabletop Simulator deck, a spreadsheet or card images, whether on the New game page or on a new game’s empty table, now opens the same step-by-step page as the home page: where to find your file, what to check, and the importer beside it. Back to the table takes you to your new game."},{date:"2026-10-11",tag:"fix",title:"Your own pieces stay on the board",body:'Dropping a chess or board-game piece on a square held by one of your own pieces used to "take" it off to a tray. Only an enemy piece is captured now.'},{date:"2026-10-11",tag:"improvement",title:"Playing on a phone",body:"Held upright, the table now turns to fill the screen and sits in a frame like the web’s. Who’s at the table runs across the top, and one button at the bottom moves the game to its next step. Tap a card in your hand to play, flip or discard it, and tap a row like the board to read it up close."},{date:"2026-10-11",tag:"improvement",title:"Phones ask what brings you here",body:"Open Prototable on a phone for the first time and it asks whether you’re designing a game or here to play. Designers see what it does, the plans and a one-tap sign-up; players get the classic tables and a box for a friend’s table code."},{date:"2026-10-11",tag:"improvement",title:"A home screen for your phone",body:"On a phone, Prototable opens on your tables: the game you’re working on, with a button to its table, and any table you joined that’s still going, with Rejoin. Under them, three classic tables and Browse. Your game’s cards, ideas, notes and the rest are in the table’s More."},{date:"2026-10-11",tag:"feature",title:"Download a copy of your game",body:"On a paid plan, your game’s overview has a Download a copy button. It saves everything in the game as one zip: your cards (as a spreadsheet too), templates, every image you uploaded, rules, decks, notes, playtest records and your table, so your work is yours to keep."},{date:"2026-10-11",tag:"improvement",title:"Publishing checks your game’s name",body:"Your game can be called anything while it’s yours. When you publish it, its name and tagline go on a public page, so a name with language we can’t show there asks you to change it first, and one that uses a well-known brand like Pokémon waits for a quick review before it goes public."},{date:"2026-10-11",tag:"improvement",title:"Updates arrive quietly",body:"A new version of Prototable now installs itself while your tab is in the background, and your table is just as you left it. If you’re at the table when one is ready, a small note in the corner offers it instead of a bar across the bottom."},{date:"2026-10-11",tag:"improvement",title:"Design a game or play one",body:"The first time you open Prototable you start at the table, which asks whether you’re designing a game or here to play, then shows just that: the card editor and the ways to bring your cards in, or Blackjack and the other classic tables. The home page is a click away, and there on your next visit."},{date:"2026-10-11",tag:"fix",title:"Card text wraps in every language",body:"Japanese, Chinese and Thai text on a card used to run off the edge, because lines only broke at spaces. It now wraps where words end in any language. Hebrew and Arabic text starts at the right edge of its field, on cards, table labels and chat bubbles."},{date:"2026-10-11",tag:"feature",title:"Accessibility settings",body:"Settings has a new Accessibility section. High contrast strengthens faint text and borders and makes see-through panels nearly solid, in light or dark. Text size makes every label and menu larger. Colorblind-friendly player colors, a clear keyboard focus ring, and Reduce motion, which stops animations and makes the camera jump rather than glide, are there too."},{date:"2026-10-11",tag:"fix",title:"Find your way back to your table",body:"Signed out, leaving your table for the home page used to hide it from the sidebar until you’d made a card, so the only way back looked like starting over. Your game and its table now stay at the top of the sidebar once you’ve been there, with any tables you’ve joined right under it."},{date:"2026-10-10",tag:"improvement",title:"Next runs every classic table",body:"Texas Hold’em now deals itself from the bar: Next shuffles for the blinds, deals, goes round the seats for each round of betting, and turns the flop, turn and river. In Chess, Checkers and Crazy Eights, Next passes the turn, and in Chess it switches the clocks too. Timer steps can now swap two clocks, for your own games."},{date:"2026-10-10",tag:"feature",title:"Steps for everyone, each player, or the table",body:"In Turns, each step can now say who it’s for: whoever’s turn it is, each player in seat order, everyone at once, or the table. Mark any of them and the game plays in rounds, so Next walks the whole round and each step’s automations run once a round, not once a player. Blackjack now runs this way: press Next and the table shuffles, deals, goes round the seats and plays the dealer."},{date:"2026-10-10",tag:"feature",title:"Draw until a hand adds up",body:"A new Draw until step deals cards one at a time from a pile until the cards there add up to a number on one of their Number fields, and can count a 1 as 11 the way an ace does in blackjack. The blackjack table uses it to play the whole dealer’s turn when you press Next."},{date:"2026-10-10",tag:"feature",title:"Clear a lane in one step",body:"Macros and automations have a new Move all cards step. It moves everything in a pile or lane, each player’s own, or every lane on the table, onto another pile, so a cleanup phase is one step instead of a card at a time."},{date:"2026-10-10",tag:"feature",title:"Learn a classic game at the table",body:"Sitting down at Blackjack, Texas Hold’em, Crazy Eights, Chess or Checkers no longer throws the rules at you. It offers a short guide instead, which points at each button and piece as you play your first hand and can press the table’s buttons for you. The rules are still one click away, and every guide is in Learn."},{date:"2026-10-10",tag:"fix",title:"Poker on the home page opens the game",body:"Poker in the home page’s sidebar led to a page saying the game wasn’t there. It now opens Texas Hold’em."},{date:"2026-10-10",tag:"improvement",title:"Starting from a file walks you through it",body:"Tabletop Simulator, A spreadsheet and Card images on the home page now open their own page: where to find your file, what to check once it’s in, and the importer right beside it. When the import finishes, your table opens with the new cards."},{date:"2026-10-10",tag:"improvement",title:"Add opens as a tray of real pieces",body:"Add now opens a slim tray: All, or one kind picked from the pills under the search, with your game’s own cards, decks, tokens and boards first. Dice, counters and pieces show as the 3D pieces you’ll get. Decks can go on the table before you sign up too, and the calculator moved to the Table menu."},{date:"2026-10-10",tag:"improvement",title:"Every field of your cards in one table",body:"The Cards table now shows a column for every field on the template, your own fields included, not just name, type, cost, effect and power. Click any cell to edit it; the change shows on the table straight away. A template with lots of fields scrolls sideways."},{date:"2026-10-10",tag:"improvement",title:"Settings moved into Publish",body:"Your game’s name, players, length and color, and archiving or deleting it, are now in Publish, under Cover and details, next to the cover and tagline they appear with. Status and the short description are gone, since nothing showed them. Overview now shows your tagline."},{date:"2026-10-10",tag:"improvement",title:"Your cards as a table, and one header on every page",body:"Build in the sidebar now opens to its tools, Data, Templates, Decks, Import, Print and export and Index cards, and each tool has a tab for each kind: Cards, Boards, Tokens and Dice. Cards, boards and tokens are full-width tables with numbered rows, and a card opens in a panel beside them. Every page’s header now lines up the same way."},{date:"2026-10-10",tag:"improvement",title:"Share your table straight from Host",body:"Before you have an account, Host and Share used to say your table needed one to get a code, with no way to get it. They now have a Sign up to share this table button, and say what you get: a code your friends join with, no account for them, ever."},{date:"2026-10-09",tag:"improvement",title:"A clearer way in for new visitors",body:"Before you have a game, the sidebar on the home page now shows the ways to start one, from a Tabletop Simulator deck, a spreadsheet, card images or a blank table, or to sit down at Blackjack or Poker, instead of an empty game. Importing opens right over your table, and the main button now says Create your own game."},{date:"2026-10-08",tag:"fix",title:"Turn automations run every turn",body:"An automation on “the turn reaches” a step now runs each time the turn lands there. In a game with one step it used to run once and never again, however often you pressed Next, and “the turn passes to someone” now runs when you’re playing alone too."},{date:"2026-10-08",tag:"fix",title:"Imports that stop halfway keep what they made",body:"iPhone photos, AVIF and BMP images, and pictures over 10 MB now import: they’re converted on the way in instead of failing on every retry. If a card sheet or spreadsheet stops partway, the cards it already made stay and it tells you where it got to, so trying again doesn’t make them twice."},{date:"2026-10-08",tag:"fix",title:"Spreadsheets import before you sign up",body:"Bringing in a spreadsheet before you had an account failed with Not found. It now adds the cards to the game in your browser, up to the free card limit, like making them by hand."},{date:"2026-10-07",tag:"feature",title:"The table on your phone",body:"Your phone now opens the table, laid out for a small screen: your hand as a row of cards, tools along the bottom, macros as chips, and a puck to turn and tilt the view. Tap a pile to draw or shuffle. Held sideways, the tools move to the sides."},{date:"2026-10-07",tag:"fix",title:"Importing images asks you to sign up first",body:"Before you had an account, importing card images or a Tabletop Simulator sheet failed with a tiny error by the button. Images are stored with your account, so the button now says Sign up to import and opens sign-up instead."},{date:"2026-10-07",tag:"improvement",title:"A shortcut for every Build tool",body:"Import is now Shift+I, Decks Shift+X, Turns Shift+T, and Print & export Shift+Z, shown beside each one in the Build menu. Index cards moved to Shift+N, and clearing a card’s targets moved to Alt+T. Change any of them in Settings, Shortcuts."},{date:"2026-10-07",tag:"improvement",title:"One table, no modes",body:"Build, Playtest and Review are no longer modes you switch between. The table stays in play, and Build (B) and Review (R) are menus beside your hand that open their tools over it. The tools on the left now have labels, and Help in the top bar holds the tours, help pages, shortcuts and bug reports."},{date:"2026-10-07",tag:"feature",title:"Help pages",body:"prototable.gg/help has the basics written down: getting started, adding cards, templates, playtesting with others, recording and review, and plans. Send Getting started to anyone you want to try Prototable."},{date:"2026-10-06",tag:"improvement",title:"New game asks it all in one window",body:"New game now opens as a window over your table and asks the name and where your cards are in one go: a card sheet, a spreadsheet, card images, or from scratch, or start from a blank table or a deck of playing cards. Create opens the right tool on the new game straight away."},{date:"2026-10-06",tag:"fix",title:"Deleting a game works again",body:"Delete in Game settings did nothing for any game that had ever been opened on the table. It now deletes the game, closes its tables, and takes you back to the game you were on before. Archiving and deleting games come with a plan."},{date:"2026-10-06",tag:"improvement",title:"A new game asks where your cards are",body:"A brand-new game used to open on an empty table with nothing saying where to begin. It now asks where your cards are, and opens the right tool: a Tabletop Simulator deck, a spreadsheet, card images you already made, or a first card from scratch. You can also go play a classic first."},{date:"2026-10-06",tag:"fix",title:"The turn stays with the right player",body:"When someone joined a game in progress, or got up from their seat, the turn bar could jump to a different player. The turn now stays with whoever has it, however people come and go."},{date:"2026-10-06",tag:"feature",title:"Playtest goals",body:"Press Record and you can jot down what this playtest should tell you, like “does the market feel too cheap?”. They sit by the Record button while you play, you mark how far each got when you end the session, and Playtest review shows what every playtest set out to answer. Only you see them, and the open ones carry over to your next recording."},{date:"2026-09-30",tag:"feature",title:"Your rules, as a book on the table",body:"Put a Rulebook on the table in Edit mode and players can double-click it to read your rules like a real book, open where it lies or full screen, turning its pages for the whole table. It shows exactly what’s in the Rules window, so the two always match. Pick a hardback, paperback, spiral notebook or journal, and add pictures to your rules."},{date:"2026-09-28",tag:"fix",title:"Attachments stay on cards in lanes",body:"A clip, marker or cube on a card vanished when the card went into a lane. It now rides along, and you can attach things to a card that’s already in a lane, from its menu or by dropping a token on it."},{date:"2026-09-28",tag:"feature",title:"Macros that ask how many",body:"In a macro’s step, tick Ask for this number each time and give it a name: pressing the button then asks for it, filled in with the usual amount. In the command box, type the numbers after the macro’s name in order (upkeep 3 1) or by name (upkeep cards 3 coins 2). A macro with nothing marked works as before."},{date:"2026-09-28",tag:"improvement",title:"Give a macro a number: deal 3",body:"In the command box (/), type a macro’s name and a number to run it with that number this once: deal 3 draws three where the macro usually draws two. It changes the macro’s first draw, put-back, add, set or bag draw, and the box shows what it will change before you press Enter. deal 3 * 2 runs it twice."},{date:"2026-09-28",tag:"improvement",title:"Automations in the command box",body:"Press / and type an automation’s name: Enter pauses it or turns it back on. The same search offers running its steps right now, without waiting for it, and opening it to edit. Type automations to see them all."},{date:"2026-09-28",tag:"feature",title:"A command box for the table",body:"Press / to run anything by name: your game’s macros, and every command that has a shortcut (open Players, start recording, switch to Build), with the shortcut shown beside it. ⇧F still puts things on the table, and now takes an amount and a color: 3 red cubes, blue hex, green d20 or cube * 5 puts down just that, side by side."},{date:"2026-09-28",tag:"feature",title:"Attach things to cards",body:"Right-click a card and Attach a clip, a marker, a cube or a counter. It rides on the card: it moves and turns with it, goes into your hand with it, and comes back when the card is played. Drag a clip along the card to slide it down an HP track on its edge, or drag anything off the card to take it away. A token dropped on a card attaches the same way."},{date:"2026-09-28",tag:"fix",title:"P shows and hides projections again",body:"With Project every card on, P on a selected card seemed to do nothing: it switched the card’s own projection while the automatic one stayed put. P now shows or hides whatever you see over the card. The card menu also no longer runs off the bottom of the screen."},{date:"2026-09-28",tag:"improvement",title:"Grid mats size their tokens",body:"A grid mat can make flat tokens fill its cells: add one onto it, or drop one on it, and it takes the cell’s size, centered. It’s on for new grids (Tokens fill cells, in the mat’s Grid settings) and can be turned off to keep tokens as they are."},{date:"2026-09-28",tag:"fix",title:"New pieces land on the table",body:"Adding a piece while pointing past the rail, or with the view panned off the edge, used to drop it out on the floor where nobody could reach it. It now lands just inside the table’s edge."},{date:"2026-09-28",tag:"improvement",title:"Hex tokens, and change a token’s shape",body:"Tokens come in a hex shape, for hex maps, alongside round, square and triangle ones in Add, and token templates can be hexes too. Right-click a plain token to switch its shape (round, square, triangle, hex, disc, cube, pawn or meeple) or its size, and on a grid mat, Fit cell makes a flat token as big as the cells. A mat’s Grid settings can fit every token on it at once."},{date:"2026-09-28",tag:"feature",title:"Hex and square grids on mats",body:"A mat can be printed with squares or hexes (pointy or flat top) in Edit: pick the cell size, the line color and strength. Pieces, dice and cards dropped on it settle in the middle of a cell, and pieces still stack, so a map for a wargame or a tile-laying game works out of the box. Lines can be any color, and a mat can be see-through, so the grid lies right on the felt. Everyone at the table sees the same grid."},{date:"2026-09-28",tag:"fix",title:"Keep and Discard follow you",body:"Keeping or discarding a card change in Review is saved to your account, so a change you reviewed on your laptop is reviewed on your iPad too. The number on Review now counts only the changes still waiting for you."},{date:"2026-09-28",tag:"feature",title:"Hear from your players",body:"When a recorded playtest ends, everyone who played gets a short card: how was it, and a few questions (what was the best moment, was anything confusing, would you play again) until you write your own. Their answers are in Review under Players said, session by session, and in each session’s recap. Change the questions, or turn on a mid-game pulse, at the bottom of Players said."},{date:"2026-09-27",tag:"improvement",title:"Design notes moved to Build",body:"Your pages of notes are now Design notes, in Build beside Index cards, where the designing happens. Review is left to what your playtests told you, and the rules players read live in Rules."},{date:"2026-09-27",tag:"feature",title:"How each card is playing",body:"Review has a Cards view: every card’s record across your recorded playtests, how often it came into play and every note about it, with what’s worth a look first at the top: cards that never come up, cards up in every game, the most talked about, and the ones you’ve changed since you last tested. Open a card to see it session by session, and whether it’s been played since your last edit."},{date:"2026-09-27",tag:"feature",title:"Notes on a card, mid-game",body:"While a playtest is recording, right-click a face-up card on the table or one in your hand and choose Add note. Tag it balance, idea, feel or bug; it’s filed against that card, so it’s waiting for you in Review next to everything else said about it."},{date:"2026-09-27",tag:"improvement",title:"Browse, tidied",body:"Browse opens as a window beside your table instead of covering it, and the classic tables have new artwork. Your own table no longer asks how you’re playing: that’s asked on a published game’s page, where players arrive, and stand-ins are still in the Players window."},{date:"2026-09-27",tag:"improvement",title:"Show your game as a box",body:"In Publish, under your cover, you can have Browse show your game as a game box, cover on the front and title down the spine, instead of a flat picture."},{date:"2026-09-27",tag:"feature",title:"Play solo, or with others",body:"Before you sit down, pick how you’re playing: just you (you play every seat, and switch between them from your hand), with friends at a table of your own, or with anyone. Every published game asks on its page, so it’s how players come to your game too."},{date:"2026-09-27",tag:"feature",title:"Classic tables",body:"Texas Hold’em, blackjack, chess, checkers and Crazy Eights are ready to play from Browse, with their rules, and macros and automations doing the dealing, the chess clock and the dealer’s hole card. Live now always has an open table: Play sits you with someone waiting, or starts one for you to share. They’re built with the same tools you have, so open one to see what yours can do."},{date:"2026-09-26",tag:"improvement",title:"Copy and paste on the table",body:"Cmd+C and Cmd+V (Ctrl on Windows) copy whatever you have selected and paste it where you’re pointing, as many times as you like. In Edit they copy a pile, lane, mat or camera, settings and all. They replace Shift+X; typing and copying text work as they always did."},{date:"2026-09-26",tag:"improvement",title:"Change several counters at once",body:"Counters, dials and health bars can join a selection now: Shift-click them or drag a box around them, then press + or − on any one and they all move together, each within its own limits. The selection panel has Up, Down and Reset for them too."},{date:"2026-09-26",tag:"feature",title:"Rules at the table",body:"A Rules button in Playtest opens your rulebook beside the table. Split it into tabs, color them, and write in markdown; everyone playing can read along and search for a word, and your edits reach them as you save. Players see the rules when they first join, which you can turn off in Publish."},{date:"2026-09-26",tag:"feature",title:"Your badges",body:"Settings has a Badges tab: every milestone you’ve reached making and testing games, from your first card to a hundred playtests, and the ones still ahead."},{date:"2026-09-26",tag:"feature",title:"Prototable on your phone",body:"On a phone, Prototable opens to your ideas, cards, rules and card designs, each full screen, so you can write a card or jot an idea the moment it comes to you. It’s waiting on your table when you’re back at a laptop or iPad, which is where you lay out the table and playtest. On smaller laptop and tablet screens, the top bar now folds its less-used buttons into a menu instead of crowding."},{date:"2026-09-26",tag:"improvement",title:"Six at a free table",body:"Free accounts now seat 6 players at a table instead of 4, so a full game night fits before you ever think about upgrading."},{date:"2026-09-26",tag:"improvement",title:"Plans, side by side",body:"Upgrade now opens a Plans window over your table, like any other window: Free, Designer and the Studio plan that’s coming, with what each one adds, and the limits compared side by side underneath. Every price is what you pay for the year."},{date:"2026-09-25",tag:"improvement",title:"Simpler projections, with health bars",body:"Select a card and press P to show or hide its projection (with nothing selected, P still pings). Its settings now read top to bottom: everything on the projection as a list you can change in place, one row of things to add, then how it looks. Any number on a projection can be a health bar that empties and changes color as it drops."},{date:"2026-09-25",tag:"fix",title:"Pinned counters stay on top of their card",body:"A counter, dial, or health bar dragged onto a card now lies flat on the card, like a token on a real one, instead of hovering above it, and it stays on top while you carry the card. Before, a carried card lifted straight through it."},{date:"2026-09-25",tag:"feature",title:"Health bars",body:"Add a Health bar from the Add menu: a slim bar about a card wide that empties as the number drops, going amber under half and red under a quarter. Press its left or right end to take or give a point. Drag it onto a card and it rides along with that card, so a creature can carry its own health. Any counter can switch to a bar under Look in its settings."},{date:"2026-09-25",tag:"feature",title:"Counters and dials know where they start",body:"Every counter and dial now has a Starts at number in its settings: a life dial starts at 20, a score at 0, or whatever your game needs. Reset puts it back there (a dial used to drop to 0), and Reset table starts every counter over for a new game. For every new one, right-click any counter and choose Defaults: one window sets how new counters, dials, and timers start in your game, limits and all. Macros and automations get a Reset counter step, for one counter or all of them, and their counter steps now respect a counter’s lowest and highest."},{date:"2026-09-25",tag:"fix",title:"Your table size sticks",body:"Setting a table size, then reloading, could shrink the table back to its old size and throw your layout off, and other players at the table kept seeing the old size. The size you pick now holds through a reload and reaches everyone at the table straight away."},{date:"2026-09-25",tag:"improvement",title:"Open to everyone",body:"You no longer need to join our Discord to use Prototable: sign up and you’re straight in. The Discord is still where designers share games, ask questions, and hear what’s coming, and it’s one click away beside the theme button."},{date:"2026-09-25",tag:"improvement",title:"New macro steps pick a pile with cards in it",body:"A Draw, Shuffle, or Flip step used to start on the first pile on the table, which was often an empty one, so a new macro failed the first time you pressed it. It now starts on the first pile that has cards in it. You can still pick any pile."},{date:"2026-09-24",tag:"fix",title:"Deleting lots of cards at once works",body:"Selecting a big batch of cards in Data and pressing Delete or Archive only got through the first 60 or so, and said nothing about the rest. It now handles the whole selection in one go, however many you pick, and tells you how many it changed."},{date:"2026-09-24",tag:"feature",title:"Learn: short tours you do, not read",body:"Learn in the top bar lists short tours, each one a single thing done for real on the table you’re at: move and flip cards, roll dice, or bring in a Tabletop Simulator deck. Search for what you want, or browse by what you’re trying to do. Any step can be done for you with Show me, and a tour that adds a die asks at the end whether to keep it."},{date:"2026-09-24",tag:"improvement",title:"See at a glance when your game is live",body:"Once other people can play your game, the Publish button in the top bar reads Live with a green dot, so you can tell without opening it. Hover it to see whether it’s listed on Browse or only open to people with the link. Menus in the top bar, like the game switcher and Host and Join, now open over any windows you have open instead of behind them."},{date:"2026-09-23",tag:"feature",title:"TTS keys, and sets of keys to switch between",body:"Settings → Shortcuts has a Keys menu at the top. Pick the TTS layout and the table answers the way your hands expect: Q and E only rotate, the arrows move the camera like WASD, P switches between seated and top-down, and Enter says something where you point. Change any key and it’s saved as your own set, so you can keep a few and flip between them; the originals are always there to go back to."},{date:"2026-09-23",tag:"improvement",title:"From card sheet to real cards in one click",body:"After a card sheet import, Read all reads every card’s fields straight through and stops only for you to check the values before anything is saved. Rebuilt cards keep the colors of the originals — a cost in its colored circle, colored stats, the frame — and a card that’s only a picture no longer shows form fields that don’t appear on it."},{date:"2026-09-23",tag:"feature",title:"Turn card pictures into real cards",body:"Cards you imported as images — a card sheet, a folder of scans — can become cards you edit. Select them in Data and choose Read fields: it works out what changes from card to card and where it sits, shows you the boxes over the card to check, then reads every card’s name, cost, type, text, and stats into a table you can correct. Make the cards, and they’re rebuilt on a template in that layout, with the art cut from each card’s own picture. The original picture stays on the card, and each card’s previous version is saved in its history. Part of the paid plan."},{date:"2026-09-23",tag:"fix",title:"Your hand does what you point at",body:"Clicking a card in your hand sometimes did nothing: after you dragged a card onto the table, the next click was lost. And the area that responds to your pointer didn’t match the cards — with one card in hand, part of it wouldn’t react and a patch of table beside it did. Both are fixed, and the card under your pointer now lights up with an outline, so you always know which one a click will pick."},{date:"2026-09-23",tag:"feature",title:"Import cards from a card sheet",body:"Import is its own tool in Build now, and it has a new way in: a card sheet — one image with a grid of cards on it, like a TTS deck or a print-and-play sheet. Say how many cards across and down (10 × 7 is one click) and how many are on it, check the cut lines over the preview, and each card becomes a card in your data. Deck lists moved to the Decks window, since they make a deck and the other imports don’t. The free plan’s image uploads go up from 60 to 200, enough for a whole game’s art, and one import can bring in 70 images instead of 25, so a whole sheet comes in at once."},{date:"2026-09-23",tag:"improvement",title:"Decks, easier to find your way around",body:"The Decks window matches the rest of the app now. Every deck sits in a row of tabs across the top, with New deck always in view beside them — it used to hide at the bottom of the deck name’s dropdown. Click the deck’s name to rename it; the set’s cards and the deck sit side by side below."},{date:"2026-09-23",tag:"fix",title:"Switching games starts clean",body:"Windows and the macros and automations columns you had open for one game used to follow you into the next. Switching games now closes them, and each window reopens where you left it next time. A reload still brings back what you had open."},{date:"2026-09-22",tag:"fix",title:"A full table says so",body:"A table holds up to 200 pieces and 4,000 cards, so one runaway duplicate can’t lag everyone at it. Hitting the limit used to do nothing at all; now it tells you, and a duplicate that wouldn’t fit is refused whole rather than copying half your selection. Error messages elsewhere read as plain sentences too, instead of raw server replies."},{date:"2026-09-22",tag:"improvement",title:"More room on the free plan",body:"Free accounts now seat 4 players at a table instead of 2, and record 10 playtests per game instead of 3. Enough to bring your group, and to try two layouts and compare them in Review, before you ever think about upgrading."},{date:"2026-09-22",tag:"feature",title:"Compare layouts in Review",body:"Each recorded playtest now remembers which layout it was played on, and Review has a Compare layouts view that lines them up side by side: how long games ran, how many rounds, which seat won and by how much, and how they felt. Write down what you are trying with each layout, and what you learned once you have played it. A session where the layout changed part way through counts toward neither, so the comparison stays honest."},{date:"2026-09-22",tag:"improvement",title:"Layouts carry the whole setup",body:"A saved layout now keeps the number of seats and the turn steps along with the table, macros, and automations, so a 2-player variant or a different phase order is something you switch to rather than rebuild. When you switch layout, or change a macro, everyone at the table gets the new macros straight away instead of after a reload. And a layout whose macros are too big to save now tells you so, instead of saving without them."},{date:"2026-09-22",tag:"improvement",title:"Counters are their color",body:"A counter used to be a black slab with a thin stripe of its color along the top, so every counter on the table looked the same. It is its color edge to edge now, on the table and in its panel, with dark or light lettering, whichever reads on it. Pick a color per seat and you can tell whose is whose across the table."},{date:"2026-09-22",tag:"improvement",title:"The scoreboard has its own window",body:"The scoreboard lived at the bottom of the Players window, where you had to scroll past the seats to find it. It has its own button in the rail now, and Shift+K, so you can open it and leave it open all game. With nothing to rank it tells you how to get something: give a counter a seat under Belongs to, and the standings line up there."},{date:"2026-09-22",tag:"fix",title:"Resize any pile by its corner",body:"A pile set to fit one card had no resize handles in Edit mode — including the main deck — so it looked like it simply couldn’t be resized. Every pile has corner handles now. Drag one on a card-sized pile and it switches to resizing freely; move it and it stays card-sized. A pile’s Kind is two buttons now, Draw deck and Discard; set a pile up any other way and it reads “Custom” beside them rather than offering a Custom button that had nothing to do."},{date:"2026-09-21",tag:"improvement",title:"A simpler pile panel",body:"A pile’s settings were ten boxes of equal weight, each a closed dropdown with a paragraph under it, so learning what a pile did meant opening nine of them. It leads with what kind of pile it is now — Draw deck, Discard, or Custom, one click — then what starts in it, then which way up its cards are, all in one place and read back to you as a sentence. What a click does, browsing, whose it is and its size fold under More, with a summary so nothing set there hides. Switching kind still asks first if it would change the cards already in the pile."},{date:"2026-09-21",tag:"improvement",title:"One list of who’s at the table",body:"The Players window listed everyone twice — once as a roster, then again in the seats below it. It’s one list now: the people actually at the table, in seat order, with their cards in hand, and the scoreboard under them. Empty seats are a single line rather than a row each, since a table seats up to twelve whether or not anyone is coming. Setting the table up — how many seats, stand-ins, where to sit — moved to a Seats tab."},{date:"2026-09-21",tag:"improvement",title:"Pin a panel where you want it",body:"Every floating window has a pin in its header, and so does the turn bar: press it and the thing stays where you put it, however much you drag at its title. Press it again to let it move. Resizing still works pinned — a window grows from its corner without leaving its place. Pins are yours alone, kept in your browser, so pinning a panel doesn’t move anyone else’s."},{date:"2026-09-21",tag:"improvement",title:"Move the turn bar",body:"The turn bar was the one thing on the table you couldn’t move. Grab it by the grip on its left and put it anywhere — where you leave it is kept, and only you see it there, because it’s about your screen rather than how the game is set up. Drop it back up top, or press the arrow, to dock it across the top again."},{date:"2026-09-21",tag:"feature",title:"A scoreboard, from the counters you already place",body:"A counter can belong to a seat now, the way a pile can — “Belongs to” is in its settings. Name one Score per seat and the Players window grows a scoreboard: a row per player, a column per counter, ranked, with a crown on whoever is in front. It is a readout, never a second set of numbers — every figure is a counter somebody placed and players click, so it can’t disagree with the table. Cards in hand sits beside each player too: you can count a fan of cards across a real table, you just can’t read them."},{date:"2026-09-21",tag:"improvement",title:"Card versions, where the cards are",body:"Saving and restoring a card version has always worked, but it lived only in the card editor’s own tab — and Data is where you read your cards now. Pick a card in Data and its saved versions are under its fields: save one, see what you have, restore any of them. A restore reaches the table straight away, and the fields you were looking at update with it."},{date:"2026-09-21",tag:"feature",title:"Do several rows at once",body:"Every row in Data has a tick beside it now, with one in the header to take everything on screen. Pick a few cards and Archive or Delete them together; pick a few boards, tokens or dice and Duplicate or Delete them. Ticks only ever cover the rows you can see, so a Delete can’t reach rows a search is hiding. Archiving also reaches the table straight away now — an archived card used to sit there until the room was reopened."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"feature",title:"Token templates have fields",body:"A token template set the look — shape, size, color — but the only thing a token could fill in was one mark, so “a template declares what varies” wasn’t true of tokens the way it is of cards and boards. A token template now names fields (Value, Cost, whatever your game needs) and every token made from it fills them in, with Data’s Tokens tab showing those fields as its columns — exactly like Cards. Drag each field around the token to place it, and size it, the way you lay out a card template; what you place is what the token shows on the table. Templates with no fields work exactly as before."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"improvement",title:"Boards and tokens start somewhere",body:"A new set has always come with a Standard card template, so you can write a card straight away instead of landing on “make a template first”. Boards and tokens didn’t — both tabs were a dead end until you went and made one. Every game now starts with a square board template and a round token template, and games you already have get them too. Delete them if they’re not your game’s: they won’t come back."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"improvement",title:"Data lists, Templates makes",body:"Data’s Boards and Tokens tabs were galleries that opened a “pick a size” wizard when a game had no template yet — template-making, in the window you came to to read a list. They’re rows now, like the Cards tab: one template at a time, a row per board or token with its own columns, and search, New, duplicate, delete and Put on table along the row. With no template yet a tab says so and points at Templates, where making one belongs. Making one there now shows up in Data straight away instead of after a refresh."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"fix",title:"A new board opens",body:"New board made one and left it sitting as a tile in the gallery you were already looking at, which read as a button that did nothing. It opens the board now, the way New token always has — except for your first, where the template opens so you can lay out its zones."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"improvement",title:"One place for templates",body:"Card templates had a window while board and token templates were tucked inside their component’s tab, so “where do I edit a template” had a different answer depending on which kind you meant. Templates is now one window with a category each — Cards, Boards, Tokens — and Data’s tabs are doors into it. Data is the other half of that: Cards, Boards, Tokens and Dice in one window, because they’re all the same kind of thing — the pieces your game is made of, each filling in a template. Components has gone; its tabs are Data’s now. Three of them are made from a template and carry its picker, and dice don’t, because a die IS its design — and it shows one template at a time, with the columns that template actually has, instead of a fixed Name/Type/Cost/Power header your cards might have nothing to do with."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"feature",title:"Draw for whoever’s turn it is",body:"A draw step can now target the player whose turn it is — the one thing a turn-triggered rule is mostly for, and the one thing it couldn’t say. “When the turn passes to someone → draw 1 from their own deck” is two clicks now. Inside a rule it’s the default, and “Whoever clicks” is labelled The host there, because a rule runs on the host’s screen and nobody clicked anything."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"improvement",title:"Automations read like macros",body:"An automation is a macro with a different trigger, so it’s edited like one now: the condition sits in the same head node a macro’s “When pressed” occupies, the steps flow from it down the same line, and it has the same Contents / Details / Behavior tabs. Rules can be named — eight of them all called “When a pile is empty” were unreadable — and the whole flow is violet, because what sets an automation off isn’t a press: it watches. They also get a column beside the tool rail like macros have, so you can see at a glance how many rules a table carries and which have run."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"feature",title:"A pile can belong to a seat",body:"Every pile now has a “Belongs to” in its settings: a seat, or everyone’s. Whoever sits in that seat owns it — and a macro’s draw, shuffle and recall steps gained a “Which pile”: that one, my own, or each player’s own. Name four piles “Deck”, one per seat, and “everyone draws 5” deals each player five from their own. Matching is by name, the way counters already work in macro text, so there’s no new vocabulary — and anyone with no pile of that name is skipped rather than dealt someone else’s. A draw step’s “Who draws” picks SEATS as well, so you can write “seats 2 and 3 draw 1” at an empty table and have it mean something when people arrive."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"feature",title:"Turns, with steps you name",body:"Build → Turns takes the steps a turn goes through — Untap, Draw, Main, End, or whatever your game calls them. The table then shows a bar across the top with the step it’s on and whose turn it is; anyone can press a step or Next, and running off the last one passes the turn to the next player (going round by SEAT now, not by who joined first). The bar appears as soon as you name a step — in Build too, so you can see what you’re setting up — and a switch beside the steps turns it off for games that want the steps for automations without a bar across everyone’s table. A macro can move the turn on, an automation can wait for a step (“when the turn reaches Draw”), and {{phase}} and {{round}} name it in chat, notes and announcements."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"feature",title:"Say something at the table",body:"Point at a spot, press C, type: what you say is pinned to the felt right there — a pin drops on the spot the way a ping does, and the bubble above it carries your name in your colour — for a few seconds, and goes to Chat like any other message. Chat was a window off to one side, so at a playtest you were either watching the board or reading the conversation."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"improvement",title:"Counters say when they’ve been pressed",body:"A counter on a projection now shows a faint minus and plus at its ends — its left half counts down, its right half up — the projection gives a nudge when a press lands, and the status line names the counter and its new value. Pressing a number and not being sure anything happened was the complaint."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"feature",title:"Targets can fade",body:"Settings → Table has a new switch: an arrow you draw between cards goes on its own after a few seconds instead of standing until someone clears it. Only your screen changes — and an arrow you drew is taken off the table once it has faded for you, so nobody is left with arrows they can’t see."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"improvement",title:"E fills the screen with a window",body:"Press E while you’re in a window and it fills the table; press it again to put it back. Over the table E still rotates what you’re pointing at."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"improvement",title:"Chat survives a refresh",body:"What was said at the table used to live only in the tab you said it in — refresh, and the conversation was gone. The table now keeps the last 200 messages, so a refresh leaves them where they were and someone joining mid-game can read back what they walked into. Resetting the table clears them (it’s a new game), and the host can clear them any time from the Host window. Nothing is lost by the cap: when a recorded session ends, its whole conversation is kept with it, and you can read it back under Sessions beside that session’s log."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"feature",title:"A Host window for your table",body:"Host, in the top bar, gathers what you do as the table’s owner: who can join (open to anyone with the link, behind a PIN, or knocking for your approval), the code and the invite link, who’s here — with the buttons to let a guest edit the table or remove someone whose seat and hand are still sitting there — and starting over, which is where Reset the table and Start a new game live now instead of a drill-down nobody found. Share has folded into it, so inviting people to your table and looking after them are one button rather than two, and joining someone else’s table with a code is its own Join button beside it."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"feature",title:"Projections can show the card",body:"A projection can float the card’s whole face, or just its art, instead of the name-and-numbers panel — handy when the table is full and you want to see what something actually is. Settings → Table sets what projections show by default — switch it to The card and every projection becomes one — and any card can be set differently in its own settings, or put back to Default. There’s a Both as well — the card above its panel. Counters can sit over the picture, in a strip below it so they never cover the art, or be hidden, and they’re pressable wherever they are. Art uses the template’s art field, and falls back to the whole card when there isn’t one."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"fix",title:"Count on any projection",body:"Pressing a number on a projection counts it up (Shift-press counts down) even on a card you never set up by hand. A number that belongs to one of the card’s fields changes the card itself; anything else pins the projection so the count has somewhere to live."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"feature",title:"A projection on every card",body:"Settings → Table has a new switch: every card on the table shows its name, numbers and text, without setting them up one at a time. It’s per device, so a player who finds them cluttered leaves it off, and you can hide any single card’s from its right-click menu. Projections also show a card’s text fields by default now, not just its numbers."},{date:"2026-09-20",tag:"improvement",title:"Flip a card in your hand, play it face down",body:"Point at a card in your hand and press F to turn it over — the flip key used to look only at the table. A card you’ve turned face down then plays face down, whether you drag it out or play it from its menu."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"fix",title:"Cards keep their facing in a lane",body:"Dropping a face-down card into a lane turned it face up. A card now arrives the way it already was, and a lane has its own Face up or down setting if you want it to insist — as it was, always face up, or always face down."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"fix",title:"Chat, Escape, and WASD",body:"Chat follows the newest message again (and leaves you alone while you scroll back through history). Escape clears a half-typed message, and the next Escape closes chat. Holding WASD while dragging the table with the mouse now moves the camera instead of doing nothing."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"fix",title:"New decks show up in pile settings",body:"A deck you imported or built while the table stayed open didn’t appear in a pile’s “use a saved deck” list until you reloaded. The table now picks up new decks as they’re made, and refreshes the list whenever you open a pile’s settings."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Paste any SWUDB export",body:"Star Wars: Unlimited deck lists now import from all three SWUDB exports — JSON, Melee, and Picklist — plus plain lists of card names. Cards without a set number are looked up by name, a line we can’t match is reported instead of dropped, and sideboards are left out so a 50-card deck imports as 50 cards."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"fix",title:"Sign-in and the Discord check",body:"Signing in from www.prototable.gg left you apparently signed out; www now goes to prototable.gg, where the session belongs. And the Discord membership check no longer takes your access away when Discord can’t be reached or your Discord link has gone stale — it refreshes the link, and the screen offers to sign in with Discord again."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Game settings from the game menu",body:"The game name menu has a Game settings row: name, short description, color, status, and the player count and play time your public page shows. It opens over whatever you’re doing, so you can fix a typo in the name without leaving the table."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"fix",title:"No stuck card when switching seats",body:"Switching to another seat while your pointer rested on a card in your hand left that card’s big preview stuck on screen. It now closes when the card is no longer in the hand you’re playing."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Foil playing cards",body:"The deck of playing cards takes foils now. The finish goes on that one copy, so you can have a single shiny nine of hearts in an otherwise plain deck, and it stays with the card through shuffles and reloads."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"improvement",title:"An easier foil picker",body:"Foil on a card now opens a grid of swatches grouped into Rainbow, Glitter, Metal, and Special, with one line describing the finish you point at, instead of a long list. The foil dropdowns in Card data, card overrides, and template settings are grouped the same way."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"feature",title:"Seven more foils",body:"Prism (a diamond lattice that flips color), Ripple (rings rolling out from the middle), Confetti (scattered flakes that flash), Pinstripe (sliding rainbow lines), Pearl (a soft pastel shimmer), Neon (the art’s edges glow), and Textured (the art looks raised). Neon and Textured work from your card’s own art, so they suit busy illustrations best."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"fix",title:"Projections ride with their card",body:"Picking up a card with a projection used to leave the projection behind at the card’s old spot, and the lifted card rose straight through it. The projection now travels with the card while you carry it."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"feature",title:"Four new foils",body:"Rainbow Rare washes the whole card in a pastel rainbow, Galaxy scatters rainbow stars over a violet nebula, Silver turns the light areas to mirror chrome, and Starburst fans rainbow rays out from the middle. Pick them from Foil on a card or in a template’s Settings, and tilt the view to watch them move."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Colored projection counters",body:"Each counter on a card’s projection can have its own color: press the dot beside its name and pick one, so health reads red and resources gold at a glance. Light puts it back to the projection’s own color."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Middle click to ping",body:"Click the middle mouse button on the table, or Shift+right-click, to ping that spot for everyone, the same as pressing P. Dragging with the middle button still moves the camera."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Target several cards at once",body:"Point at a card and press T, then click the card or piece it targets. Shift-click to target several in a row; a plain click pins the last one and stops. Point at a card and press Shift+T to clear its targets. Target arrows also keep a visible point when you look straight down at the table, where they used to end in a dot."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"fix",title:"Shift+A opens Add again",body:"The shortcut on the rail’s Add button did nothing while the table had focus, which is nearly always. It works now, and you can rebind it in Settings → Shortcuts."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Inspect cards on hover",body:"Settings → Table has a new Inspect cards on hover switch. Turn it on and resting your pointer on a card opens its details, so you can read a table full of cards without clicking each one. It’s off by default and only changes your screen."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"feature",title:"Play every seat yourself",body:"Testing a four-player game alone? Open Players and add stand-ins under Play every seat yourself: each fills an open seat. Press Play on a stand-in to take its hand and its view, N for the next seat, and Back to you on the hand bar to return. Remove a stand-in and its hand goes back to the deck."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"feature",title:"Playing cards, chess, and your own pieces in Add",body:"The Add menu and the right-click search now offer a shuffled deck of playing cards, a full chess set on its board, and single chess pieces, alongside the dice, tokens, and boards you designed for this game. The playing cards belong to the table, not your game, so they never show up in your card data. Designed boards now draw on the 3D table too."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"feature",title:"Design your own dice and tokens",body:"Components now has Dice and Tokens tabs. Give a die its own colors and put a word, a symbol, or art on any face (Hit, Miss, Crit), or duplicate one for a variant. Tokens work like cards: a token template sets the look (shape, size, colors, art), and each token in its family sets its own mark or art in a table, so Damage 1, 3, and 5 are one template. Put them on the table from Components or the Add search, and edits update the copies already in play."},{date:"2026-09-19",tag:"improvement",title:"Cards sit centered in lane cells",body:"A card that isn’t the size its lane was cut for (a Bridge card in a Poker lane, say) now sits in the middle of its cell instead of tucked in a corner. Lane settings have a new Where a card sits in its cell choice if you’d rather line them up to an edge."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"improvement",title:"Layouts carry your whole table",body:"A saved layout now includes your cameras, macros, and automations along with the piles and mats, and switching to one brings them all back. Pin a layout with the globe in Table settings and every public game (and new room) opens with it, macros included."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"improvement",title:"prototable.gg on card backs",body:"Designed card backs on free plans now carry a small prototable.gg mark along the bottom edge, under your back text. Paid plans don’t, for everyone in the room, and backs you upload as an image are never marked."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"improvement",title:"Foil on a whole template",body:"Set a foil in a template’s Settings and every card on it gets that finish, so a Rare template can simply be foil. Any card can still pick its own finish, or None to stay plain on a foil template."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"feature",title:"Foil cards",body:"Give any card a foil finish: Holo, Sparkle, Shattered, Gold or Etched. Pick it from Foil on a selected card, in Card data, or under the card’s overrides in Build. On the 3D table the foil shifts as you move the view or pick a card up, foils in your hand catch the light as your pointer moves across them, and the card previews lean toward your pointer so you can tilt it to catch the light."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"improvement",title:"Projections follow a card’s numbers",body:"A projection on a card whose template has Number fields now shows those numbers, as they stand on that copy. Click one on the projection to step it and the card changes too, and changing it on the card updates the projection. The projection’s settings list every field on the card with a checkbox: tick numbers to show them as linked counters and text fields to show them as lines under the name."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"feature",title:"Targets: arrows that stay",body:"Draw an arrow from one card to another card or piece and it stays, in your colour, following both as they move — this unit attacks that one. Right-click a card for Target… to do it without switching tools. Draw the same arrow again, or click it with the arrow tool, to take it away; a target also goes by itself when either card leaves the table. Arrows from or to the felt still fade like before."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"improvement",title:"Arrows arc over the table and snap to cards",body:"Arrows used to lie flat on the felt, so cards and piles covered them. They now leap from one point to the other in an arc drawn over everything, and start or end exactly on a card or piece when you press or let go on one — a quick way to show one card targeting another."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"feature",title:"Shift+N for a quick idea",body:"Press Shift+N anywhere to jot an idea down before it goes. Enter files it as an index card and the note gets out of your way, with a quick Saved to confirm; Esc closes it. Esc also now closes the front floating window, once anything it was busy with (a menu, a selection) is dealt with. Sessions moved to Shift+J to make room."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"feature",title:"Automations that watch card numbers",body:"An automation can now fire when a card’s number drops to a value — “when a card’s Health drops to 0” — once for each card, and its new Move that card step can send that card to the discard. Macros and automations can also reset card numbers, all of them or one field, and the card panel has a Reset numbers button."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"feature",title:"Change a card’s numbers on the table",body:"Number fields on your templates can now be changed on each copy in play: point at the number on the card and it outlines, then click its right half to add one or its left half to take one away. Clicking anywhere else on the card just selects it. Only that copy changes, never the card’s design, and a changed number turns red when it’s below what’s printed and green above it. A card that goes back to a hand or a pile comes out with its printed numbers again, and right-click has Reset numbers."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"fix",title:"Card edits no longer lose a field",body:"Editing a card on the table saved only the last field you typed in if you moved on quickly, so a new card’s name could vanish while the form still showed it. Every field you touch is saved now."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"improvement",title:"Take a chosen piece out of a bag",body:"Select a bag and each kind of piece inside it has a Take out button, for the times a rule lets you look in and choose rather than draw blind. The log says you took one out, so nobody mistakes it for a draw."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"feature",title:"Macros and automations reach bags, timers, and text",body:"A macro can now draw pieces from a bag, start, pause or reset a timer, and rewrite a text or heading on the table — with the same {{variables}} as announcements, so a heading can read “Round {{counter:Round}}”. Automations can watch for a bag running empty or low, and for a timer running out. A button macro can also move your view — to a camera, your own seat, or the whole table — so a “Start combat” button can take you to the battlefield."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"feature",title:"Text on the table, and images on mats",body:"Headings and text are part of setting up the table now, like mats: add them from Edit, drag to move, drag a corner to resize, and write the words, pick their colour, and give them a background or none. Players can read them but not knock them about. Mats can carry an image too — a printed playmat, a board, a player aid — from the Image section of the mat’s settings; it fills the mat and the mat’s name steps aside for it."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"fix",title:"Timers and notes are back on the table",body:"Timers and text notes had quietly stopped showing up on the 3D table. They are real objects on it now: a timer is a slab with its countdown floating above it, and a note is a sticky note with your words printed on it. Select one to start, pause and reset the timer or set its length, or to write the note. Everyone sees the same countdown."},{date:"2026-09-18",tag:"feature",title:"Bags",body:"Add a Bag from the Add panel, drop tokens, cubes, meeples or marbles on it, and double-click it to draw one out at random — the room picks, so nobody can choose what comes out. A bag shows how many pieces are inside, and taking a bag off the table tips its pieces out rather than losing them."},{date:"2026-09-17",tag:"improvement",title:"Floating card projections",body:"Card projections now use light rings and a floating readout, with readable names from every angle. Right-click a card to adjust the height and color, or track up to four named counters without changing the printed card."},{date:"2026-09-17",tag:"feature",title:"Import a Star Wars: Unlimited deck by pasting it",body:"Paste a deck list from swudb — text or JSON — and the cards arrive as a deck, named, with quantities, ready to play. It sits in Import cards under “From a deck list”, and in the deck builder, where it also builds the deck for you. The images are fetched once at import and saved to your account, so nothing depends on anyone else’s site while you are playing. Leaders and bases come in the right way round — they are printed sideways, so they get their own landscape template — and a leader’s deployed side arrives as its own card, since a leader is landscape one way up and portrait the other."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"improvement",title:"Settings is a window now",body:"Account, billing, preferences and shortcuts opened as a dialog in the middle of the screen, which is not how anything else in the app opens. It is a floating window like the rest — drag it, resize it, leave it open beside the table while you change something and watch it take effect."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"feature",title:"Change any keyboard shortcut",body:"Shortcuts open as their own window from the table menu or Shift+/, and the same list is in Settings. One thing worth knowing: snap-to-grid moved from G to Shift+G, because G also groups the cards you have selected and both were happening at once. Settings has a Shortcuts tab listing every shortcut the app has, grouped by what it does and saying where each one works. Press a shortcut to change it — you press the keys rather than naming them — and it tells you if something else already uses that combination. Reset one, or all of them, whenever you like. The shortcuts shown on buttons and in menus come from the same place, so a shortcut you changed reads correctly everywhere instead of the app still advertising the old one."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"improvement",title:"The number keys are your views now",body:"Press 1 to 9 to jump to a seat or a camera, and 0 for the whole table. Seats come first, so the low numbers are the people at the table, and everyone in the room gets the same key for the same view. Switching between looking down at the table and sitting at it moved to the backtick key, since it is a once-a-session decision and the numbers are not. The View menu also stopped listing your cameras — the column beside the rail lists them, and both lists saying the same thing was the confusing part. That column holds seats as well now, with the key for each one written on it."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"improvement",title:"One button for how you are looking at the table",body:"The compass, the two turn buttons and the View button were four rail buttons for one idea, sitting next to each other. They are now one: a compass needle badged 2D or 3D, whose menu holds the 2D/3D switch, straighten and fit, the ninety-degree turns, and your cameras and seats. The bracket keys still turn the table without opening anything."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"improvement",title:"Your cameras are there while you play",body:"The cameras column beside the rail is no longer just for setting them up. In Playtest it is the same list without the editing controls: press one and the table goes there. Automations also sit next to Macros in the rail now, since both are things the table does for you."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"feature",title:"Drag a corner to frame more or less of the table",body:'There was no way to say "show me less than that" — a camera framed whatever its height and tilt happened to give it, and the dots on the corners of its shot were decoration. Pull a corner in and the camera comes closer to what it is looking at; push it out and it stands back. It keeps looking at the same spot from the same direction the whole time, so it is the one control for how much of the table a camera takes in.'},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"fix",title:"Dragging a camera’s target turns it, instead of dragging the camera",body:"The target on the felt is meant to be what the camera looks at, but moving it carried the camera along with it, so the whole thing slid sideways rather than swinging round. The camera now stays exactly where it stands and turns to look at the new spot, its view stretching or shortening to reach it. Drag it too far and the target stops rather than the camera tipping past level."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"fix",title:"Letting go of a camera no longer makes it jump",body:"Releasing a camera snapped it back to where it had been and then jumped forward again once the table confirmed the move. It now holds where you put it until the table agrees, so there is nothing to see. The camera you are editing is also picked out in orange in the list, so the list and the table say the same thing."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"improvement",title:"One camera at a time, and a target you can see",body:"Every camera drew a marker for where it looked, whether or not you were editing it, so several cameras appeared to be in play at once. Only the one you are editing shows its view and its handles now; the rest are a body and a name that brighten when you point at them, which is how you switch. And the spot a camera looks at is a filled target rather than a hairline circle, so it reads as something you can pick up and move."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"improvement",title:"Three handles, and a proper look at the table",body:"The big green arrow is gone, along with the rest of the borrowed 3D gizmo. Its arrows pointed along fixed directions, which mean nothing to a camera aimed any which way, and the one that did read took over the screen. A camera has three handles now and each does one thing: grab the camera to move it around, its ring on the felt to change what it looks at, and the ball above it to raise and lower it. Opening cameras also stands you back from the table at a side angle, so you can see where each camera’s view actually lands."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"fix",title:"A camera shows its view, not a drawing of it",body:"The camera had a cone on its front meant to show which way it looked, and the cone pointed the wrong way — so the camera appeared to face one direction while everything else about it faced another. It is gone. The camera you are editing now draws the box it actually sees, on the felt where it lands, with four lines running out to its corners from the lens. Every part of it comes from the same four points, so no piece of it can contradict another."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"fix",title:"A camera’s outline now matches what it sees",body:"The shape drawn on the felt for the camera you are editing was a rectangle around its view, worked out at the main window’s proportions — so it sat nowhere near where the camera was actually pointed. A tilted camera sees a trapezoid, near edge short and far edge long, and that is what it draws now, at the same proportions as the picture in the corner. Turning a camera is one ring as well: the second one turned it about a fixed direction, which means something different depending on which way the camera already faced, so there was no way to aim with it. Editing cameras puts you back in the 3D view too."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"fix",title:"Picking a camera in the list actually picks it",body:"Pressing a camera beside the rail lit its tile up and did nothing else — no handles on the table, no preview, and the controls under the list had nothing to act on, because the table was never told which camera you meant. It is now. The controls have labels rather than bare icons, and the camera you are editing draws the rectangle it frames on the felt, with a dot at each corner, the same way a pile or a mat shows its shape in the plan."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"improvement",title:"Place cameras from the floor plan",body:"Editing cameras used to tip the whole table into 3D, on the grounds that a camera has height and a floor plan does not. The preview in the corner already shows what the camera sees, so there was nothing to gain: the table stays flat and keeps the same simple drag handles everything else on it has, and the preview is the 3D view. It is bigger now, and sits clear of the Chat and Log buttons."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"improvement",title:"Cameras live beside the tool rail",body:"The floating Cameras window is gone. Cameras are a column beside the tool rail now, the same as macros: one tile each, a plus to add another. Pressing a tile picks that camera — its handles appear on the table, the preview follows it, and its controls appear right under the list: look through it, put it where you are looking, whether the handles move or turn it, and its settings. The settings panel only turns up when you ask for it instead of every time you glance at a camera. Opening cameras puts the macros column away, since they share the spot."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"improvement",title:"Just grab a camera and drag it",body:'Moving a camera meant finding one of several small coloured arrows, which is a lot to ask for "put it over there". Grab the camera itself and drag it, or drag the ring on the table to change what it looks at — and watch the preview while you do. The handles are still there for exact work, cut down to the two that mean anything: a flat square to slide it over the table and an upright arrow to raise it.'},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"feature",title:"See what a camera sees while you place it",body:"Picking a camera in Edit table now shows a live picture of its view in the corner of the screen. Move it, turn it, change its height, and the picture follows — so placing a camera is watching the shot rather than reading a wireframe and imagining it."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"improvement",title:"Cameras look like cameras",body:"A camera used to draw the whole cone of what it could see — four lines out to the corners of its view — which for a low camera reached clean off the table and read as a thicket of wires rather than an object you could pick up. A camera is now drawn as a camera: a small body with a lens pointing the way it looks, a ring on the felt where it is aimed, and a thin line to the floor so you can tell how high it is. The drag handles are smaller too, and the technical settings are folded away under Adjust by hand — most of the time you just move the table to the view you want and press Put the camera where I am looking."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"improvement",title:"Editing cameras is its own mode now",body:"Arranging a table and placing a camera want opposite things from the view — one is a floor plan seen from straight down, the other cannot be judged without height — and doing both at once meant piles, mats and cameras all grabbing at the same click. Opening the Cameras window while Edit table is on now puts you in 3D with only the cameras live: everything else on the table holds still. Close it and Edit hands back to the floor plan. The camera settings appear in one place instead of two, and the window tells you when Edit is off rather than quietly doing nothing."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"fix",title:"Camera handles no longer drag the table with them",body:"Grabbing a camera handle also started panning the table, so the table slid out from under the handle and nothing went where you put it. The table now stands down the moment a press lands on a handle. There is also a simpler way to place a camera that skips the handles entirely: move the table to the view you want and press Put the camera where I am looking."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"feature",title:"Set how many seats your table has",body:"Every room came with eight chairs whether your game was for two or for twelve, and there was no way to change it. The Players window now has a seat count, one to twelve, and changing it re-seats everyone’s table straight away without a reload. It is also the number of people who can be in the room at once, so it is the one dial for table size, and it will not let you shrink below the people already sitting down."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"feature",title:"Change seats, and take your view with you",body:"Every seat at the table is listed under the View button now, taken or open, so moving round to sit beside someone is one click from wherever you are rather than buried in the Players window. Taking a seat somebody is already in swaps the two of you, so nobody loses their place and nobody has to ask. And your view goes with you: sit down somewhere new and the table turns to face you from that chair, which is the point of sitting next to someone in the first place."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"feature",title:"Cameras have a real gizmo, and every seat has a camera",body:"Placing a camera by dragging it across a flat floor plan never worked — a camera is a thing in the air, and the view it was being placed from had no height in it. Pick a camera in Edit table now and the view tips back up into 3D and puts a gizmo on it: arrows to move it, rings to turn it, the same handles any 3D tool gives you. Look through still works, and leaving it puts you back exactly where you were instead of stranding you wherever the camera pointed. Seats have views too, now, derived from where each player sits — nobody sets them up, every seated player just has one. The View menu has a switch at the top: Cameras for the angles you saved, Seats for the people at the table, and Home always takes you to your own."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"fix",title:"Camera bodies follow your cursor when you drag them",body:"A camera floats well above the table, but dragging one measured your cursor against the table instead — so the thing you had hold of was never under your pointer and never caught up with it. A camera now drags on a plane at its own height, and stays where you grabbed it rather than jumping to the cursor. The bodies are bigger, their name is part of the grab, and what a camera looks at is a ring on the felt instead of a small cross."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"feature",title:"Look through a camera to aim it",body:"Edit table lays the table out flat from above, which is the right way to arrange piles and mats and exactly the wrong way to judge a camera — a tilt is invisible from straight down. Open a camera and press Look through: the view becomes that camera, and every move of the table from then on is a move of the camera, saved as you go. Esc puts you back. Only the camera you are working on draws the cone of what it sees, too; four cameras all drawing theirs at once was a thicket of lines across the whole table."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"feature",title:"Cameras are objects you can see and move",body:"A camera used to be a rectangle drawn flat on the table, which meant it could only face one of four directions and had no idea how high it was or how far it looked down — so saving an angle quietly straightened and flattened it. A camera now holds the view it was saved from, exactly: where it looks, which way it faces, its tilt, its distance. Turn on Show cameras and they appear on the table as real objects, floating where they stand, with the cone of what each one sees drawn down onto the felt and a marker on the spot it is pointed at. In Edit table you drag the camera itself to walk it around its subject, and drag the marker to change what it looks at; its height and tilt are numbers in the camera panel. Adding a camera now takes the view you are looking at, so you fly the table where you want it and press Add. Cameras from before this keep working — they show up where their old rectangle put them."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"feature",title:"Seats are seats, cameras are cameras",body:"Sitting down at the table used to mean the designer first drawing a rectangle for every side — seats and saved camera angles were the same object, so a room with nobody to set it up had no seats at all. Every table now comes with a full set of chairs: one per player the room allows, arranged around its edges, with the long sides getting more of them and the first two facing each other. You are seated the moment you join, and the Players window shows who is where — take an open seat, stand up, or, if you are running the room, move anyone to any chair. Cameras go back to being what they sound like: angles you save and jump between, with no one sitting in them."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"fix",title:"A card in your hand no longer stays stuck selected",body:"Picking a card in your hand lit it up, and clicking the table to change your mind did not put it out again — the table let go but the hand did not, so the card sat there selected with no obvious way to clear it. The hand now follows the table: click the felt, or pick up anything else, and your hand card lets go with it."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"improvement",title:"The game log says who, and filters by what",body:"Turning a card, adding a pile, moving a seat — none of those ever recorded who did them, so the log said “Someone” even when you were alone at the table. The server now notes who sent every action, so new entries name the player. Entries already written stay as they were, since nobody recorded it at the time. The log also has filters along the top — Cards, Piles, Table, Players — for when a busy game buries the one thing you are looking for, and a download button that saves what you are looking at as a text file: one line per action, timestamped, readable on its own or handed to an AI to ask what went wrong."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"fix",title:"The put-something-here search looks like everything else",body:"The search that opens where you right-click had its own flatter, more opaque panel and showed a chunky scrollbar down its side. It now uses the same glass as the other floating menus, and the list scrolls without the bar."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"feature",title:"A game log beside chat",body:"Your table now keeps a timestamped record of everything that happens on it — cards drawn and played, piles shuffled, recalled, added and deleted, layouts applied, players coming and going — whether or not you are recording a session. Open it with the Log button next to Chat, or Shift+G, and filter it by card, player, or action. It is the thing to reach for when something looks wrong and you need to know what actually happened three rounds ago. Yours alone: the log names every card drawn, so players do not see it. Kept for two weeks, and you can switch it off per game under Publish → At the table. Public rooms follow the recording switch instead: if a game records its public playtests, their tables are logged too, and if you turn recording off, nothing is logged there — the people playing were told they were being recorded, so the log keeps the same promise."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"fix",title:"Decks no longer pour into each other",body:"A pile that draws from a saved deck could quietly swell with cards that were never part of it. Editing a card while the table was live poured the whole set into whichever pile happened to be first, every time, and returning a hand to the deck sent every card to that same pile no matter which deck it came from — so a nursery of 12 could end up holding 94. Cards now go back to the pile they actually came from, and a pile bound to a deck only ever holds that deck’s cards. Piles you have already mixed up stay as they are: use Recall on each one to sort them back."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"improvement",title:"Tap a card in your hand to look at it first",body:"On an iPad, tapping a card in your hand played it straight onto the table, so there was no way to look at a card without committing it. The first tap now selects the card — it lifts, and its panel opens beside the table — and tapping that same card again plays it. Dragging a card out still plays it in one motion, and a mouse works exactly as before."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"feature",title:"Reset the table from the Build toolbar",body:"Build has a Reset table button in the tool rail now. It re-seeds every pile from your decks, reshuffles them, and clears the felt, so a table that has drifted while you were designing goes back to a clean start in one press. It also empties everyone’s hands, so it is a between-games button rather than a mid-playtest one. Yours alone, as the designer of the table."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"improvement",title:"Double-click a pile to draw from it",body:"Taking a card off a deck meant dragging its top card away, which is the fiddliest thing on the table and hard work with a trackpad, a shaky hand, or a finger. Double-click any pile — or double-tap on a touch screen — and its top card goes straight to your hand. Dragging still works, and a pile you have set to draw on a single click behaves as it did."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"feature",title:"Call a deck’s cards back",body:"Recall is on the 3D table now, the way it always was on the old one. Select a pile (or right-click it) and Recall pulls every card that belongs to that deck back into it, from the table, out of hands, and out of other piles. It is the quickest way to tidy up after a game, or to round up cards that ended up somewhere you cannot reach. Piles that do not own any cards do not offer it. Hover a pile and press R to call its cards home without opening anything, and the cards themselves fly back across the table and land on the pile — the ones that actually moved, so three coming home looks like three. Everyone playing sees them go, not just whoever asked for it."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"fix",title:"Deleting a pile no longer flings its cards off the table",body:"Removing a pile tipped its cards out at the far corner of the table instead of where the pile stood, so they landed in one overlapping block off the edge of the felt that nothing could reach. Everyone else did not see them move at all: the cards still pointed at a pile that no longer existed. They now land face up where the pile was, for everybody."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"fix",title:"Get rid of cards stranded on the table",body:"A stack left behind by an import could sit off the edge of the felt with no way to remove it: every button in the selection panel rearranged the cards instead of clearing them. Selecting cards now offers Remove from table, and a single card has Remove in its panel. The cards themselves stay in Build, so this only clears those copies from that table."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"improvement",title:"Switch between 2D and 3D with a key",body:'Press 1 to look straight down at the table and 2 to sit at it, the same two views the compass menu offers. The floating "Editing table" tag is gone too: the Edit table button already turns orange and reads Done while you are editing.'},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"feature",title:"Put something on the table, fast",body:"Right-click the table (or hold a finger on it) and a search opens for anything you can put there: a card from your game, a counter, a dial, dice, a token. Pick one and it lands on the spot you pressed. Shift+F opens the same search and places it wherever you are pointing. Piles, lanes, mats, and seats are table setup, so they appear only if you can edit the table: placing one lands it in the middle of your view and opens it in Edit, where you move, resize, and configure it."},{date:"2026-09-16",tag:"fix",title:"Dragging cards from your hand stops selecting text",body:"Pulling a card out of your hand could start a text selection instead, highlighting the labels around it, and on an iPad a held card could raise the text callout and magnifier. The hand now treats every press as handling a card."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"fix",title:"The frame width slider works in 3D",body:"Dragging Frame size did nothing on the 3D table, which always drew the same rail. It now widens and narrows the wooden edge as you drag, and never disappears entirely, since a table needs an edge. New tables also start on blue felt with the grid off."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"improvement",title:"Felt and frame join Table & view",body:"The window that held the grid is now Table & view, and it has the rest of how your table looks: the felt colour (with your own colour if you want one), the cloth weave, and the wooden frame with its width. All of it is per device, like the grid, so it never changes the game for anyone else."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"feature",title:"Join a table with a code",body:"Invite is now Share, with two things under it: invite people here, which copies the link and shows the room code in big type so you can read it out, and join with a code, for when someone gives you one. Paste a code or a whole link and you land at their table. It works signed out and on an iPad, where there was no way in before."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"improvement",title:"Edit table is a switch, not a mode",body:"Editing the table now has its own Edit table button beside the mode menu, and it works from Build, Playtest, or Review. Turn it on to move and resize piles, lanes, mats, and cameras, then press Done (or Esc) to land back where you were. The mode menu is Build, Playtest, and Review on Shift+1, 2, and 3, and Shift+E turns editing the table on or off. Templates moves to Shift+Y."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"feature",title:"Turn, flip, and stand up counters and dials",body:"Counters and dials now handle like everything else on the table. Q and E turn them, F flips one over to hide its number until you flip it back, and Stand up props one on its edge facing you, so you can hold up a life total for the table to read. Find all three in the right-click menu and the selection panel."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"feature",title:"Dials",body:"Add a Dial from Counters & Trackers: a round life dial with its number in a window at the top, − and + either side, and its name across the middle. Like a real one, its numbered wheel spins under the cover to bring each new number into the window. Click its left side to turn it down and its right side up, and select it to rename it, set its limits, or recolour it. Counters are also smaller now, so they sit beside cards without crowding them."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"improvement",title:"Counters, dice, tokens, and piles get the card panel",body:"Selecting a counter, die, token, or pile now opens the same panel a card does, instead of a strip of buttons along the top. A counter shows large with its numbers, name, limits, and colour; a die shows its face with Roll and its type; a pile shows its count with Draw, Shuffle, Browse, and Peek."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"feature",title:"Counters you can use on the table",body:"Counters on the 3D table are now score blocks you play with: click the − or + end to step them (Shift-click for five), drag them anywhere, and select one to type a value, rename it, or set its step and a lowest or highest value. Right-click for reset, colour, lock, and remove. A counter can also track several numbers at once, like 2/2 for power and toughness or wood · stone · gold: add numbers with + Number, name each one, pick what goes between them, and click a number to choose which one − and + change. Good for scores, points, round trackers, and health totals."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"improvement",title:"New games start in Build",body:"Your first visit, and every game you create, now opens in Build, where you make the cards, instead of on an empty table in Playtest."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"improvement",title:"A lighter welcome",body:"Your first visit now starts with a simple glass menu: jump into your own prototype or explore community games. Less reading, more room to play."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"improvement",title:"Table windows on the rail for players",body:"Players in a public room now open Table, Physics, Players, and Cameras from the tool rail, the same place you find them at your own table, instead of a Table button in the corner."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"fix",title:"Selected dice show on mats",body:"A selected die or token sitting on a mat now shows its selection ring on the mat, instead of the ring hiding underneath it."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"improvement",title:"Friendlier table on iPad",body:"Hold a finger on a card, pile, die, or token to open its menu, the same one right-click gives. While you carry something, a second finger pinches, pans, and turns the table under it. And small things like dice and tokens are easier to grab: a fingertip that just misses still picks them up."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"improvement",title:"Try everything before you sign up",body:"Without an account you can now add cards from Card data and design your own templates. They stay in your browser and come with you when you sign up. New visitors also start in dark mode on a gray slate table."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"feature",title:"Live now on game pages",body:"When a designer turns on List live rooms, their game's page shows the public rooms running right now, with how many are playing and a Join button, so you can jump into a game in progress instead of starting your own."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"feature",title:"Allow editing in public rooms",body:"Public rooms are play-only by default, even for you. Turn on Allow editing in public rooms under Publish, Settings to get Build, Edit, and Review in your game's public rooms, for you and any players you give edit rights. Card edits made there change your real cards."},{date:"2026-09-15",tag:"improvement",title:"Public rooms are for playing",body:"A public room is a copy of your game for playing, even when it is your own. Inside one you play like everyone else: no Build, Edit, or Review, and no card or table edits. Leave the room and edit at your own table."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"fix",title:"Everything stays on the table",body:"When your table has a set size, mats, piles, lanes, dice, and tokens can no longer sit past its edge. Anything already hanging off moves back on when you open the table, a mat bigger than the table shrinks to fit, and anything added while you were looking past the edge lands inside."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"feature",title:"Layers in Edit",body:"Edit mode has a Layers tool listing every mat, pile, and lane from the top of the table down. Drag them or use the arrows to decide what sits on top, say one mat over another or a lane under a mat. Mats start at the bottom, so nothing changes until you restack."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"improvement",title:"Pick a mode from a menu",body:"The mode button beside your game name now opens a menu of Build, Edit, Playtest, and Review, and each has a shortcut: Shift+1 to Shift+4 (Tab still cycles). Changes waiting for review show on the Review row instead of an unexplained badge. Browse moves to Shift+B."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"improvement",title:"Right-click cards in your hand",body:"Right-click a card in your hand to play it face up or face down, or put it straight onto any pile, without dragging it across the table."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"feature",title:"End a public room",body:"The LIVE pill in a public room now has End room, for you or whoever is hosting, which ends the recording and closes the room for everyone, and Back to my table to return to your own table. Public rooms also close on their own after 30 minutes without a move, with a two-minute warning, and players see that the room has ended instead of just dropping out."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"improvement",title:"Browse and Publish open over the table",body:"Browse and Publish now open as windows on top of your table instead of replacing it, so closing them puts you right back where you were. Open a game from Browse and its page shows in the same window, with a way back to the list."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"feature",title:"Browse piles on the 3D table",body:"Right-click a pile to Browse it or Peek at the top card, then draw a card, put one on the table, or rearrange the order. In Edit, a pile can also be set to draw its top card when clicked, so a deck works like reaching for the next card, and to keep cards dragged onto the table face down until someone flips them."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"improvement",title:"Table setup moves to Edit",body:"Table size and collision now live in Edit, under Table settings, alongside layouts and presets, since they set the game up for everyone. Playtest keeps Grid & view for your own grid on this device. If a public room hits your recorded session limit, the table now says so instead of quietly not recording, and an acting host can start and stop recordings too."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"feature",title:"Record public playtests",body:"Public rooms now record a session as soon as someone joins, even a single stranger playing alone, so you can see how new players get on in Review. Just sharing a game to play with friends? Turn off Record public playtests under Publish, Settings. Rooms you invite people to still only record when you press Record. Players always see a note when a session is being recorded for you."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"improvement",title:"Each mode brings its own tools",body:"Switching modes now swaps the tool rail on the left. Build shows your card data, templates, components, decks, print, index cards, and import; Edit shows Add, cameras, and table settings; Playtest keeps the table tools and windows; Review shows the review, notes, and sessions. The Table menu in the corner is gone, and Report a bug sits at the bottom of the rail. Hover any rail button to see what it is. Chat moved to the bottom right, out of the way."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"improvement",title:"Modes beside the game name",body:"The mode button now sits next to your game's name at the top: click it (or press Tab) to step through Build, Edit, Playtest, and Review. The corner button is the Table menu again, and it lists what the mode is for: your cards and templates in Build, the piles, lanes, mats, and cameras on the table in Edit. Entering Edit turns the view top-down and centres the table; leaving it puts your view back."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"improvement",title:"Tab switches modes",body:"Press Tab to step through Build, Edit, Playtest, and Review (Shift+Tab goes back). The mode menu opens as you switch, so you can see where you landed."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"improvement",title:"Settings and Add that fit the mode",body:"Pile, lane, mat, and camera settings now match the table's windows: soft sections, pill choices, and round color swatches. The Add panel follows the mode too: in Playtest it offers counters, dice, and pieces; in Edit, text, piles, lanes, mats, boards, and cameras."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"feature",title:"Edit the table",body:"Edit is now a mode next to Build, Playtest, and Review. Pick it to move and resize piles, lanes, mats, and cameras right on the 3D table: drag one to move it, drag a corner to resize (snapping to the grid when snap is on), and click one to open its settings in the top right. Cards, dice, and pieces fade and stay put while you edit, so the layout stands out. Switch back to Playtest, or press Esc, when you're finished."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"improvement",title:"Build, Playtest, and Review in one corner button",body:"Build, Playtest, and Review moved from the top bar to one button in the bottom right, beside your hand. Press it to open that mode's menu, press again to step to the next mode, or pick one from the tabs at the top of the menu. Every row now has its own icon."},{date:"2026-09-14",tag:"improvement",title:"One View button for cameras",body:"The two camera icons in the tool rail are now one View button. Press it to switch between 2D and 3D, jump to the whole table or any of your cameras, and save the current angle to a camera. Who sits where moved to a Seats section in the Players window, so the Cameras window is just about angles. Chat is no longer repeated in the Table menu; it lives in the bottom-left bar."},{date:"2026-09-13",tag:"improvement",title:"Contents and Settings when editing a panel",body:"Editing a macro or panel now splits into two tabs. Contents is the actual work: its action buttons and their steps. Settings holds the name, look, who can see it, and what its button does. New panels open on Settings so you can name them first."},{date:"2026-09-13",tag:"improvement",title:"Panels open as floating windows",body:"Panels set to open as a window now sit in the macros column beside one-click macros, marked with a small window badge. Click one to open it as a floating window on the table, or right-click to edit it."},{date:"2026-09-13",tag:"improvement",title:"Edit macro steps beside the panel",body:"Adding or editing a step in a macro or automation now opens its own panel docked beside the one you're building, instead of a full-screen dialog, so the rest of the macro stays in view. The step editor now looks like the other table windows, and macro settings are laid out as clear sections: name and description, look, who can see it, and the rail button. Scrollbars across the table's windows are thinner and match your theme."},{date:"2026-09-13",tag:"improvement",title:"View controls in the tool rail",body:"Turning the table, the compass, top-down or seated, and cameras are now icons in the tool rail on the left. Press the compass to straighten the table. One camera button steps through your cameras and the whole table; right-click it to list them all, with claim and save-view options."},{date:"2026-09-13",tag:"fix",title:"No more black flash when the window resizes",body:"Opening or closing a browser sidebar, or resizing the window, no longer flashes the 3D table black while it resizes."},{date:"2026-09-13",tag:"fix",title:"Decks open fast in big games",body:"The deck builder's card list now shows 60 cards a page with Previous and Next, instead of drawing every card at once. Games with thousands of image-heavy cards open right away, and search still looks through the whole set."},{date:"2026-09-13",tag:"improvement",title:"Hide your hand on the 3D table",body:"Hiding your hand now also hides your own cards at your seat on the 3D table, and stays hidden next time you open the game. A new Show card count switch under More hides how many cards you hold without hiding the hand itself."},{date:"2026-09-13",tag:"fix",title:"Print sheets for big games",body:"Print sheets with thousands of cards no longer crash the page. The preview now draws only the pages near where you are scrolled, PDF export uses far less memory, and sprite exports split into 10x7 sheets instead of one image too large to save."},{date:"2026-09-13",tag:"feature",title:"Right-click menus on the 3D table",body:"Right-click anything on the 3D table for its options. Pieces: change colour, lock, remove, and knock pawns and meeples over onto their side (everyone sees them topple) or stand them back up. Dice: roll, switch between d4 and d20, or recolour. Cards: flip, rotate, peek, or put in your hand. Piles: draw or shuffle."},{date:"2026-09-13",tag:"feature",title:"Dice and selection on the 3D table",body:"Dice are now real numbered 3D dice, from d4 to d20. Click one to select it, then press R (or Roll) to toss it. Pick one up to move it without changing its number, shake it and let go to throw it, or hold Shift and pull back to slingshot it across the table (pull farther to shoot harder): it bounces off the rail and other dice, and whatever face lands on top is the roll. Everyone at the table sees it land. Add any die size from the Add panel. Whatever you click now gets an outline, and hovering shows a faint one. Click a pile to Draw or Shuffle it, or drag its top card to play. Pile and lane labels now face you from any angle. The Add panel works while the 3D table is open: new dice, tokens, and marbles land where you're looking, and tokens and marbles show up as 3D pieces. Drawing, arrows, pings, and the ruler are greyed out in 3D for now instead of closing it."},{date:"2026-09-13",tag:"improvement",title:"Move around the 3D table like a real one",body:"Drag or Shift-drag the table to pan, right-drag to spin it all the way around (it settles square when you let go near straight), Option-drag to turn it in 15° steps, and press [ or ] to turn it 90°, just like the 2D table. Hold Shift and use the arrow keys to tilt the view (↑ ↓) or spin the table all the way around (← →). Scroll to zoom toward your cursor. The compass in the corner straightens and fits the table in one click, with 90° turn buttons beside it. On iPad, pinch to zoom and twist to rotate. Hold WASD to glide, and Q/E zoom when no card is selected. The table now uses your felt colour and table frame, with piles labelled by name and card count."},{date:"2026-09-13",tag:"improvement",title:"A smoother, lighter 3D table",body:"Decks and discards in the 3D table now look like real piles and show the correct top card. Other players' moves glide into place instead of redrawing the whole table, face-down cards no longer load their faces, and big games stay light, with no limit on how many cards are shown."},{date:"2026-09-12",tag:"fix",title:"Bring cards back to your hand",body:"Dragging cards back from the 3D table now uses the full hand area, including when your hand is empty. A highlighted drop area shows when releasing will add the card to your hand."},{date:"2026-09-12",tag:"feature",title:"Try the live 3D table",body:"Explore your table in top-down or seated views with clearer card faces and an upright card inspector. The experiment now uses your existing sidebars, seat controls, and live card actions, including lane automations and undo. The same hand fan works in both views, and picked-up cards lift, lean, and settle as you move them. Cards enlarge upright as you pick them up, then flatten and shrink to table size as you move farther onto the board. Overlapping cards now rest above one another without cutting through their faces. The table shares the main controls, supports WASD panning and Q/E card rotation, and displays your custom card backs. Restoring a move no longer replays its lane effects."},{date:"2026-09-12",tag:"improvement",title:"Move between seats",body:"Visit seat views without changing who has claimed them, return to your seat, or frame the whole table from one compact strip. Designers can save the current framing for a seat; Alt+Shift+1–9 visits seats and Home returns to yours."},{date:"2026-09-12",tag:"feature",title:"Connect card plays to table rules",body:"Automations can now watch for a named card entering a pile or lane. Use a card play to change counters, announce effects, or run your existing action steps. Counter-at-most conditions also let you enforce a minimum score or resource value."},{date:"2026-09-11",tag:"improvement",title:"Clearer playtest controls",body:"Log in directly, use draw-deck and discard presets, and see what a macro will do and whether it finished. Dependent pile steps wait for the table, ending a session opens its history, and playtest notes can start a linked card revision."},{date:"2026-09-11",tag:"improvement",title:"Review what you learned",body:"Review now brings session notes, wrap-ups, and how the playtest felt to the front—even when no cards changed. Save experiments for the next playtest and start again from a session’s end checkpoint."},{date:"2026-09-11",tag:"improvement",title:"Choose pile cards by their pictures",body:"The starting-card picker now shows card thumbnails alongside full names, making artwork and variants easier to tell apart. Selected cards are highlighted, and cards assigned to other piles stay clearly marked."},{date:"2026-09-11",tag:"improvement",title:"Clearer pile settings",body:"Pile settings now use compact menus and plain-language explanations for starting cards, browsing, dropped cards, and size. Each setting explains what your current choice does, so it is easier to set up a deck or discard pile."},{date:"2026-09-11",tag:"improvement",title:"Let full lanes grow",body:"Lanes can now add columns or rows when more cards arrive, instead of overlapping them inside the original space. Choose the behavior under “When full” in lane settings; expanded lanes keep their size when cards leave."},{date:"2026-08-23",tag:"improvement",title:"Big card sets stop bogging down the gallery",body:"The Cards gallery used to draw every card in the set at once, so a few hundred image cards could crawl or crash the tab. It now draws what you're looking at and releases the rest as you scroll — a 400-card set holds about a twentieth of the memory it did, and scrolling stays smooth however big the set gets. Bulk image imports also no longer lose a whole run when part of it fails partway: the images that uploaded become cards, and only the ones that actually failed are left for you to retry. Live tables got the same treatment: the server no longer re-sends your whole card catalog to every player each time someone shuffles or draws, so big games stay responsive mid-playtest."},{date:"2026-08-12",tag:"feature",title:"Give your game a board",body:"In Arrange mode you can now frame the play area: set the view you want and hit Frame this view, and everyone's table opens fitted to that board instead of wherever their browser last left the camera. Card size stops being something you fiddle with — it falls out of the board fitting the screen. Panning stays free, a Fit to board button snaps you back, and the padlock next to it holds everyone on the board. Games without a frame keep the infinite canvas exactly as it was."},{date:"2026-08-11",tag:"feature",title:"See what actually happened in a playtest",body:"Review now opens a session with a What happened panel: how many cards were played and drawn, which cards came up most, and — the useful one — which cards never came out at all. It reads the real play history rather than a written log, so the numbers are the game's, not a summary of it."},{date:"2026-08-06",tag:"fix",title:"Players get the table look controls too",body:"If you joined someone else's table, the custom colour, felt texture and table frame options were missing — the player view had drifted behind the designer's. They're all there now, and the frame is no longer limited to whoever owns the room."},{date:"2026-08-06",tag:"fix",title:"The grid and felt cover the whole table",body:"The grid — and the felt weave with it — only painted a patch near the middle of the table until you panned or resized the window. Both now cover the table from the moment you open it."},{date:"2026-08-06",tag:"fix",title:"Drag cards out of your hand on iPad",body:"Dragging a card from your hand to the table now works by touch — pick it up and drop it exactly where you want it, lanes included, the same as with a mouse. Tapping a card to play it works too, in both the fan and the browse strip."},{date:"2026-08-06",tag:"feature",title:"Jump to your seat",body:"Claimed a camera? A new button above the rotate controls sends the view straight to it — zoomed to fit and turned the right way up for that seat, so a player sitting across the table isn't reading everything upside down. It only appears once a camera is assigned to you."},{date:"2026-08-06",tag:"feature",title:"Remove a stray card from the table",body:"Right-click any card on the table and choose Remove from table to clear that copy out of the room — useful when a layout dealt cards loose instead of into a pile and you want to re-deal cleanly. It asks first, and the card itself stays in Build untouched. Only the host or someone with edit access can do it."},{date:"2026-08-06",tag:"improvement",title:"Arrange mode: resize, and a layer legend",body:"Arrange now resizes as well as moves — grab the corner of any pile, mat, board or camera. It also shows a Layers panel listing piles & lanes, mats & boards, and cameras, with a count and an eye toggle for each. Hide a kind to get at whatever it was sitting on top of — overlapping cameras and lanes used to make the one underneath impossible to grab. Labels also read properly on a light table now."},{date:"2026-08-06",tag:"fix",title:"Deleting a card clears it from your table",body:"A card you deleted in Build used to stay on the live table — and in the deck, and in players' hands — until you closed and reopened the room. It now disappears from open rooms straight away."},{date:"2026-08-06",tag:"feature",title:"A dashboard for your game",body:"Build opens with a Dashboard: what's in your game at a glance, its details, and a Getting it playable list showing what's still missing — templates, cards, a deck, rules, a session, cover art. Every unfinished step links straight to the place that does it, and nothing is ticked by hand: the list reads your actual game."},{date:"2026-08-06",tag:"improvement",title:"Make a new set without leaving Build",body:"The set dropdown above your cards can now create one. Sets are mostly for organising a big game into parts — a base set and an expansion, say — and each one carries its own cards and templates."},{date:"2026-08-06",tag:"fix",title:"Two of your games can no longer share a name",body:"Naming a new game the same as one you already have is now refused with a clear message instead of quietly creating a second identical row in your game switcher. Renaming follows the same rule."},{date:"2026-08-06",tag:"improvement",title:"Your hand can sit in the edge of the table",body:"A new Hidden below edge control in the hand's More panel tucks the fan into the bottom of the screen, the way the big digital card games do, so your board gets the space instead. It starts at a slight 10% tuck; push it further and hover a card to read it in full."},{date:"2026-08-06",tag:"improvement",title:"Clicking a card no longer takes over the sidebar",body:"Selecting a card on the table just selects it now — the card panel stays out of your way while you move pieces around. Alt+click (Option on a Mac) opens the panel when you actually want it, and the pin in the panel's header brings back open-every-time if you preferred it."},{date:"2026-08-06",tag:"fix",title:"The tour waits to be asked",body:"The walkthrough used to open itself on your first visit, landing on top of the welcome window. Now an arrow points at the tour button in the left rail and you start it when you want it."},{date:"2026-08-06",tag:"feature",title:"Lock the zoom on your table",body:"Size your board how you want it read, then hit the padlock beside the zoom controls — the scale stops moving in both directions, so a stray trackpad pinch can't shrink your table to nothing. Panning stays free, and the lock remembers the level you set for next time. Click it again to zoom freely."},{date:"2026-08-03",tag:"feature",title:"Cover art and a tagline for your game",body:"Upload key art for your game on the Publish page and drag it to pick the framing — the crop you set is the crop players get on your Browse card and at the top of your public page. Add a one-line tagline while you're there. No art yet? The generated gradient stays as the fallback."},{date:"2026-08-03",tag:"improvement",title:"Publish is its own space; Browse is just browsing",body:"Browse is now the catalogue and nothing else — public games and what's live right now, on plain cards with a cover instead of a slab of felt, with no sidebar in the way. Your own game's public page moved out to a Publish button in the top bar, game settings sit in Build with the rest of your authoring, and what's-new lives behind the megaphone icon up top."},{date:"2026-08-03",tag:"feature",title:"Public tables bring the designer’s panels with them",body:"When you publish a game, the panels, one-click actions and automations you built come along for everyone who joins. Players can add panels of their own on top, but yours are theirs to use, not to change. Automations now run even when you are not at the table."},{date:"2026-08-03",tag:"feature",title:"The cards table is a real spreadsheet now",body:"Click a cell and move with the arrow keys, start typing to overwrite it, Enter to commit and drop to the next row. Select a block with shift-arrows or shift-click, then copy it straight into Sheets or Excel — and paste back the other way. A paste that runs past the last card now creates the cards it needs, so you can bring a whole sheet of card data over in one go. Cmd+D fills a selection down, Delete clears it."},{date:"2026-08-03",tag:"improvement",title:"Ideas: index cards and notes",body:"Build has a new Ideas section. Index cards is somewhere to get a thought out fast — one per card, tick it off, star it, or turn it into a real card when it earns its place. Notes moved in alongside it. There's also a peek column beside each row in the cards table, so hovering a card preview no longer covers the name you were reading."},{date:"2026-08-01",tag:"improvement",title:"A cleaner welcome, and Browse on the felt",body:"The first-visit welcome is now a single short card instead of a long scrolling pitch — one button and you're at the table. Browse moved inside the app alongside Build and Playtest, and public games now sit on green felt like cards on a real table instead of in a plain list."},{date:"2026-08-01",tag:"feature",title:"Boards are live on the table",body:"A board you place on the table is now a live object, not a flat snapshot: edit its template, name, or field values and the placed board updates in place for everyone — no more delete-and-re-add. Fill a board's fields right in the Boards data table (a column per field, like cards), and move or remove boards from the table in Arrange mode."},{date:"2026-07-29",tag:"feature",title:"Boards get the full card editor",body:"Board templates now use the same designer as cards — drag, resize, snap, layers, shapes, text, and icons — instead of the old single-rectangle tool. Each board is an instance of a template: name it and fill in its fields, and that content renders on the board. Drop a designed board straight onto the table from the Add panel's new Boards category, and delete anything from the table right in Arrange mode."},{date:"2026-07-28",tag:"fix",title:"Print sheets wait for cards",body:"Starting a print sheet before you've made any cards used to flash you into an empty onboarding screen. Now the Print & Export tab tells you to add cards first and holds off until you have some — and opening an empty sheet has a clear way back."},{date:"2026-07-28",tag:"improvement",title:"A roomier Build tab",body:"The Build sidebar now collapses to a slim icon rail, handing the extra width back to your cards, templates, and boards — hover any icon for its label, or click the toggle to bring the full list back. Card templates also open straight into a visual gallery so you can see each design before clicking in."},{date:"2026-07-18",tag:"feature",title:"Board templates, like card templates",body:"Boards now work the way cards do: design a reusable board template (its zone layout + size) once under Build → Boards → Templates, then stamp out boards from it — each board keeps the layout but gets its own name and felt color. Placing a board on the table works exactly as before."},{date:"2026-07-17",tag:"feature",title:"A Live tab — and you control what shows",body:`Running tables now have their own "Live now" tab in Browse instead of crowding the public games grid. And they're private by default: your rooms only appear in that public list once you flip "List live rooms" on in the Share settings, so a quick test session never gets broadcast.`},{date:"2026-07-16",tag:"fix",title:"Dragged cards stay on top",body:"Picking up a card on the table now always brings it above the others while you move it. In longer games it could slip underneath cards that had been moved many times — that's fixed."},{date:"2026-07-15",tag:"improvement",title:"The Build tab, reorganized",body:"Build now opens to a single left sidebar: your Components (Cards, Boards, Dice, Tokens) over your Set surfaces (Decks, Notes, Print & Export). Each component has its own Data and Templates tabs, so cards and their layouts finally live under one roof instead of two separate navigations."},{date:"2026-07-14",tag:"fix",title:"The cards table shows every template field",body:"The Build tab's table view now puts up a column for every field your template declares — not just the ones that already have data. Add a field in the template editor and its column is there waiting, so you can fill a new field straight down the sheet."},{date:"2026-07-12",tag:"feature",title:"A calculator on the tool rail",body:"Quick table math without leaving the game — a calculator lives on the left tool rail now. It keeps a tape of past calculations (click any line to reuse its result) and takes keyboard input, so totting up scores mid-playtest is a couple of keystrokes."},{date:"2026-07-12",tag:"feature",title:"The docked studio is now the default",body:"The Photoshop-style frame that's been brewing behind an experimental toggle is now the shell every designer gets: stage tabs inline in the top bar, a status bar under the table, and a right-hand dock where you can build your own panels — counters, dice, pile gauges, and one-click action buttons that stay in sync with the table. Players joining your rooms keep the same clean guest view as before."},{date:"2026-07-12",tag:"improvement",title:"Areas are now Cameras — and easier to edit",body:"The claimable seat rectangles formerly called Areas are now Cameras, because that's what they do: a player who claims one gets their view centered and rotated to face their side of the table. Cameras also got a real editor — click one in the Players & Cameras panel to rename it, set which way it faces, type an exact size, or delete it, no more pop-up prompts."},{date:"2026-07-04",tag:"feature",title:"Turn a folder of images into cards",body:'Have card art already? Open Import and switch to "From images" — drop in a batch of pictures and each one becomes a full-bleed card, no template to build. Rename any card inline before importing; the whole set uploads at once. Perfect for scanned prototypes or art you made elsewhere.'},{date:"2026-07-04",tag:"feature",title:"Group cards so they move together",body:"Select a few cards and hit Group (or press G) to attach them — they stay laid out and readable, but now move, flip, and rotate as one unit, like equipment on a creature. Click any card in a group to grab the whole thing. Tidy (T) cascades a group into a neat stagger; Ungroup (U) breaks it apart. It sits between a loose card and a stack: nothing is hidden, they just travel together."},{date:"2026-07-04",tag:"improvement",title:"Lanes lay cards out in a grid",body:"A lane can now be a full grid, not just a single row. In Lane settings set Columns and Rows — a row is 6×1, a column is 1×6, or make a 3×2 tableau, a 4×4 board, whatever fits. Cards magnetize into the cells left to right, top to bottom, and the lane sizes itself to the grid, so the old resize handle is gone. Existing lanes carry over unchanged."},{date:"2026-07-03",tag:"feature",title:"Give a player edit access to your table",body:'You can now let a trusted player help set up the table. In Game settings → Table access, flip "Can edit" for anyone in the room and they can add and edit piles, lanes, mats, seats — like a co-host. They still can’t manage who’s in the room (kicking and locking stay yours), and you can revoke it anytime.'},{date:"2026-07-03",tag:"feature",title:"Hidden lanes each player can claim",body:"Give every player a private row. In Lane settings set a lane to Backs (others see the card backs) or Hidden (others see a solid box — no cards, no count, no name). A hidden lane starts covered for everyone; a player taps it to claim it and becomes the only one who sees inside — so each seat can have its own secret staging row or battle line. As the designer you can Peek inside to set a lane up, and clear a claim from settings. Great for hidden hands, face-down reserves, or anything you reveal later."},{date:"2026-07-03",tag:"feature",title:"Table tools: point, draw, measure, arrange",body:'A tool palette on the Playtest table — click the cursor icon at the top of the rail, or hold Alt and tap a number to switch. Arrange drags every pile, lane, mat, and area at once so you can lay out a board in seconds. Pulse pings where you tap and Arrow points from one spot to another, so you can say "look here" during a live test. Draw is a freehand marker (with a one-tap Clear), and Ruler measures distances in inches. Right-drag pans the table while any tool is active, so you never lose your place.'},{date:"2026-07-03",tag:"feature",title:"Keyboard shortcuts for the whole table",body:"Drive the table without reaching for the mouse. On the Playtest table: Shift+R records, Shift+A opens Add, Shift+T the table list, Shift+P players, Shift+S game settings — and each opens ready to type. Esc closes any open panel and clears your selection. From anywhere, Shift+1–4 jump between Build, Playtest, Review, and Browse. Hover any tab or tool to see its shortcut."},{date:"2026-07-03",tag:"fix",title:"Your playtest table won’t lock you out",body:"Your game’s playtest table is your canvas, so it no longer goes to sleep on you — reopening it always brings it right back, and it keeps your layout even after an idle stretch. Rooms other people host from your published games still wind down when they’re done, as before."},{date:"2026-07-03",tag:"feature",title:"Lanes: rows that line your cards up",body:"Add a Lane from the table’s Add menu to get a labeled row (or column) that lines cards up in tidy slots. Drop a card in — from the table or straight from your hand — and it snaps into place; the others slide over, and pulling one out closes the gap. Set the slot count and switch between Row and Column in Lane settings; add more cards than there are slots and they overlap to fit, like a hand. Great for battlefield rows, tableaus, or anywhere cards should stay lined up instead of piling into a heap."},{date:"2026-06-30",tag:"feature",title:"Hover a hand card to see it big",body:"Hovering a card in your hand can now pop up a large, crisp preview so you can read it without zooming or playing it. Pick where it shows — above the card or off to the right — or turn it off, and set its size, all from the More menu in the hand bar."},{date:"2026-06-30",tag:"improvement",title:"Reorder and tidy your hand",body:"Click a card in your hand to select it (it rings blue and lifts to the front so you can see the whole thing), then step through your hand with the arrow keys to look at each card. Hold Shift and press ←/→ to actually reorder the selected card — it loops around the ends so you can keep nudging in one direction. Dragging a card into the hand is smoother too: the fan and the card you’re inserting now move together, and the drop zone hugs your cards instead of spanning the whole bottom of the screen."},{date:"2026-06-29",tag:"improvement",title:"Print sheets: back button + table view",body:'Opening a print sheet now shows a "‹ Print sheets" breadcrumb so you can get back to the list (no more dead end). And the Print & Export area honors the Table/Gallery toggle — switch to Table for a compact list of your sheets with card counts and last-edited.'},{date:"2026-06-29",tag:"improvement",title:"Mat settings panel + better framing",body:"Clicking a mat now frames the whole mat (instead of zooming in past its edit handles), and Mat settings is a real panel like Pile settings — rename the mat, pick its color, and set its size in inches right there, on top of the on-table corner handles."},{date:"2026-06-29",tag:"improvement",title:"Live card editing moves to its own panel",body:'Editing a card during a session no longer stretches the card inspector down over the table. The inspector now shows a compact "Edit fields" button that opens the form in its own panel beside it — same live, saves-to-session editing, just out of the way. (Matches how "Send to" already works.)'},{date:"2026-06-29",tag:"improvement",title:"Add text to the table, and cards mid-test",body:'The Add menu now includes Text elements — Heading, Aside, and plain Text — so you can label and annotate the table directly. And while a session is recording, a "New card" option appears in Add so you can spin up a fresh card on the fly without leaving the playtest.'},{date:"2026-06-29",tag:"feature",title:"Choose how dropped cards face",body:'Each pile now has a "Dropped cards face" setting. By default a card dropped onto a pile matches it — face-down on a deck, face-up on a discard, like a real stack. Switch it to "Keep the card’s facing" for piles where players set facing deliberately (e.g. a face-up card that should stay face-up). Find it in Pile settings.'},{date:"2026-06-28",tag:"improvement",title:"Drag cards on and off piles",body:"Grab the top card of any deck or discard and drag it straight onto the table — no more draw-to-hand-then-replay. A quick tap still does the old thing (draw from a deck, select the discard top); only a real drag peels the card off, face-down from decks and face-up from discards. Dragging a card back onto a pile is clearer too: the target pile lights up with a bold pulsing ring and the card you are holding eases down into it so you can see exactly where it will land."},{date:"2026-06-27",tag:"improvement",title:"Fixed or freeform piles",body:"Piles can now be a fixed card-shaped slot or freeform. Fixed locks the pile to one card’s footprint with a portrait/landscape toggle — no fiddly resizing — while freeform keeps the drag-to-resize handle for the rare mixed-size pile. New piles default to fixed; set it per pile in pile settings."},{date:"2026-06-27",tag:"improvement",title:"Faster pile actions",body:'Hover a deck or discard pile and a quick toolbar floats up with Draw, Shuffle, Browse, and Recall — no menu digging. Right-clicking a pile now opens a cleaner menu with icons, plus Peek at the top card and Rename. Empty a pile to spill its cards onto the table, and empty piles now read as a clear "EMPTY" slot.'},{date:"2026-06-21",tag:"feature",title:"Import and export, made first-class",body:"Bring cards and templates in or out from one place. Export your cards to CSV (opens in Sheets, Excel, or Airtable) and your templates to JSON, then import them back into any set — templates round-trip losslessly, custom fields included. Reach it from the new Import / Export button in the top bar, the Set dashboard, or the command palette."},{date:"2026-06-15",tag:"feature",title:"React with floating emotes",body:"Tap an emoji in the chat panel and it floats up across the table for everyone in the room — a quick way to react mid-play without typing. 👍🎉"},{date:"2026-06-15",tag:"improvement",title:"Chat, feedback, and the game log are one stream now",body:"The room’s side panel is a single conversation instead of three sections. Your messages show as chat bubbles, feedback keeps its tag, and table events read inline — and a message you send between sessions no longer disappears."},{date:"2026-06-15",tag:"improvement",title:"Friends who join your link get the full workspace",body:"Guests joining from a share link now see the same tabs and side rail as the app, can peek at Browse and Updates without leaving the table, and can sign up in a popup that keeps their seat — no more bouncing to a separate page."},{date:"2026-06-15",tag:"improvement",title:"Launch a game into its own tab",body:"Launching or joining a room from a game’s page opens it as a new tab beside the one you were on, so you keep your place. And if you close a room tab while others are still playing, we’ll check whether you mean to leave or end it for everyone."},{date:"2026-06-13",tag:"improvement",title:"The Updates tab tells you when there’s something new",body:"Updates now shows a dot when there’s a fresh post since your last visit, and you can close the tab when you’re caught up — it comes back on its own when the next update lands."},{date:"2026-06-12",tag:"feature",title:"Browser-style tabs: Table, Browse, Updates",body:"The app now has document tabs across the top, like your favorite editor. Your table stays open while you browse public games or catch up on what’s new — and this very page is one of the new tabs."},{date:"2026-06-12",tag:"feature",title:"Open public games as tabs",body:"Clicking a game in Browse now opens its landing page as a tab right here in the app — open a few side by side, close them like browser tabs, and your own table stays put the whole time."},{date:"2026-06-12",tag:"feature",title:"The “+” new-tab menu",body:"A “+” next to your tabs opens a card editor, template editor, or table for any set you pick — each as its own tab. Edit one game’s cards while a template for another sits a click away, all in one window."},{date:"2026-06-12",tag:"feature",title:"Keyboard shortcuts reference",body:"Hit ? (or the button in the bottom-right of the table) for a full map of the shortcuts — card rotation, pile actions, camera moves, panel toggles. No more guessing which key flips a card."},{date:"2026-06-12",tag:"feature",title:"Wrap up sessions with a mood + note",body:"Ending a playtest now asks how it felt (the same emoji scale your testers use) and takes a closing note. Both land in your session history, so scanning which playtests went well takes one glance."},{date:"2026-06-12",tag:"fix",title:"Timers stay put at any zoom",body:"Timers and labels were drifting and clipping when you zoomed the camera in close. They now scale and stay anchored exactly like everything else on the table."},{date:"2026-06-11",tag:"fix",title:"Stacked cards no longer flicker while dragging",body:"Dragging a big stack of overlapping cards made their faces strobe as the cards fought over draw order. Multi-card moves are now batched, so stacks glide as one solid block — and big drags are lighter on the connection too."},{date:"2026-06-11",tag:"improvement",title:"Lighter, steadier long sessions",body:"A deep memory audit of the table renderer: discard piles and per-card backs no longer accumulate memory over a long playtest. Multi-hour sessions on image-heavy sets should hold steady now."}],zZ="ptc_updates_seen",UZ="ptc_updates_closed";let uHe=null;const hHe=()=>uHe??(uHe=BTe().then(e=>({entries:BZ(e),latestAt:FZ(e)})).catch(()=>({entries:BZ([]),latestAt:FZ([])}))),bT="ptc:updates-seen";function sv(